1785275113189763	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwAQ		item.create	bn-3dl	{"description":"# Context & Background\nContinuum is a verification-native environment for concurrent/distributed Rust (asupersync runtime, Lean 4 proofs, TLA+ corpus oracle harness). The authoritative plan is the Revision 3 dossier at notes/plan/ — plan.md plus docs/00–55, rfcs/0001–0040, adr/0001–0052, research notes, JSON schemas, executed Python spikes, and a mechanical validator (tools/validate_dossier.py, currently: status=pass, program_status=blocked). The product thesis (plan §0.1–0.2): the product IS the proof-guided loop — protected intent → immutable snapshot → typed verification task → exact failure → bounded Context Pack → causal debugger → repair transaction → semantic/intent diff → neighborhood/mutation challenge → independent evidence → promotion receipt.\n\n# Goals\nShip Continuum 1.0 by closing release gates G0–G10 (plan §22, docs/52) through six gate-driven phases A–F (plan §21). G10 = real adoption: two materially different real systems replace bespoke DST + separate TLA+ workflow.\n\n# Structure of this bone graph\n- Child goals: Phase A–F (one per plan §21 phase) plus the §24.5 frontier-lane ratification register.\n- Phase goals are dependency-chained A→B→C→D→E→F (gate-driven sequencing, plan §21.1).\n- PR-level tasks (START_HERE_IMPLEMENTATION.md, PR 0–30 + lettered inserts 4a/15a/15b/22a/25a/26a/27a/27b) live under their owning phase. The PR sequence interleaves phases BY DESIGN — a phase closes when its gates close, regardless of PR ordinal; a PR advancing an already-closed gate is hardening, not reopening.\n- True blockers (PR 0→PR 5, PR 22a→PR 23, register ratification→phase opening PRs, etc.) are wired as bn deps, not implied by numbering.\n\n# Governing doctrine (plan §26, §21.1, START_HERE)\nClose the smallest complete trustworthy loop before broadening the verifier. No polished web UI, general LLM coordinator, distributed search, or full CML surface before the loop closes. Phases are gate-driven, not time-driven; each phase is BLOCKED until its §21.1 owner/team row is filled (a merge requirement of the phase's opening PR). A misleading assurance result, replay failure, stale receipt, hidden intent change, or unauthorized promotion is a release blocker at every gate.\n\n# How to navigate the source of truth\n- plan.md — the map (2700+ lines); §21 phases, §22 gates, §24 kill criteria, §24.5 research-lane register, §25 spec-debt ledger.\n- notes/START_HERE_IMPLEMENTATION.md — PR-by-PR implementation sequence with exit conditions (bone descriptions quote these).\n- docs/52_RELEASE_GATES_REV3.md — normative gate text (kept bullet-identical with plan §22 by the validator).\n- notes/G0_SPIKE_MATRIX.md — falsification matrix DX-01..15; freeze-blocking subset DX-01–03, 10, 12, 13, 14.\n- Where plan prose and an RFC disagree, the RFC is corrected and becomes normative (plan §25).","kind":"goal","labels":["program"],"size":"xl","title":"Continuum 1.0 — Revision 3 implementation program"}	blake3:EE3y6AJk7SShDh5wWZoMLoOjRW74w9iaYU-cmNlJhYg
1785275182923526	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwAg		item.create	bn-19p	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase A (plan §21) builds the trust spine: everything the rest of the program stands on. It owns gates G0 (falsification, freeze-blocking subset), G1 (workbench identity and lifecycle), and G2 (agent-computer interface) per the phase↔gate table (plan §22, docs/52). Opening PR: PR 0 (plan §21.1). Contains PRs 0–13 plus the PR 4a Lean band.\n\n# Goals (plan §21 Phase A deliverables)\n- Immutable workspace and intent schemas; native daemon protocol; task/continuation lifecycle; evidence graph; Context Pack v0; semantic diff v0.\n- Executable finite reference engine and certificates from Revision 2.\n- The four continuum-kernel-* crates under the docs/03 covenant (<15,000 non-test lines, no async, no unsafe, serialization boundary between engines and kernel) — phase-normative, not only a PR 9 annotation.\n- Agent protocol spike parity; Lean environment pinned, seed modules kernel-checked, T0/T1 theorems with no `sorry` and empty axiom manifests (RFC 0012 ladder, ADR-0035).\n\n# Exit criteria (verbatim intent, plan §21)\nDie Hard and Dining Philosophers check through native API, CLI, and an agent client with identical artifacts; the ACI ablation shows the typed surface beats disciplined shell use on success and cost, or the protocol is redesigned before freeze (G0-DX-10); the prompt-injection corpus cannot trigger privileged operations (G2); continuation resume validates epochs and inputs (G1).\n\n# G0 staging (plan §22)\nThe freeze-blocking subset DX-01–03, 10, 12, 13, 14 must close in Phase A. DX-01/02/03/12 already carry finite-spike evidence (artifact shapes); DX-13 closes via PR 2, DX-14 via PR 6, DX-10 via PR 10. An unexecuted or failed item in this subset blocks interface freeze.\n\n# Governance\nPhase A is BLOCKED until its §21.1 owner/team row in notes/START_HERE_IMPLEMENTATION.md is filled — a merge requirement of PR 0. Phase A also resolves: product license (permissive, asupersync/solver-compatible), corpus per-family redistribution audit, and the ADR-0029 rule that foreign oracle tooling never ships in release binaries.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nEvery later phase consumes Phase A's identities (snapshots, intents, handles, epochs), its protocol, and its trusted kernel. Getting the fail-closed spine right first is the plan's core doctrine: close the smallest trustworthy loop before broadening the verifier.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["phase-a"],"parent":"bn-3dl","size":"xl","title":"Phase A — Trust spine and ACI kernel"}	blake3:tZblUp8LDmVob8_QEQKc1O-QIag7TuRZpdGFMuXT5kM
1785275183037475	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwAw		item.create	bn-3zt	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase B (plan §21) closes the first end-to-end loop on real code: a real asupersync failure is diagnosed and repaired with a promotion receipt. Owns gates G3 (intent integrity) and G4 (causal debugging and real repair). Opening PR: PR 14 (asupersync semantic journal). Contains PRs 14–22a (minus 15b, which delivers Phase C's G5 CML-stability deliverable).\n\n# Goals (plan §21 Phase B deliverables)\n- Asupersync semantic adapter: one adapter crate; supported upstream window N and N−1 covered by a conformance corpus in CI against upstream pre-releases; upstream breaking release opens a scheduled adapter epoch (§4.6) visible in envelopes; explicit semantic-hook contract; the model core never depends on asupersync.\n- CML core-fragment parser and elaborator (Finite fragment; PR 15a).\n- Storage/network/process packs; replicated register with mutants; causal minimizer; debugger v0; repair transactions; exact and neighboring replay.\n- The incremental-engine ADR (build-vs-adopt, salsa-derived vs custom) with reuse-edge spike — Phase C is BLOCKED until this ADR exists (PR 22a).\n\n# Exit criteria (plan §21)\nAn agent fixes ack-before-durable without changing intent and produces a promotion receipt under the Phase B gate profile. Gate profiles are phase-staged: §8.2 gates 1–8 and 11–12 enter in Phase B; gate 10 in Phase C; gate 9 in Phase D. Receipts name their profile and list not-yet-enforced gates as NotYetEnforced — never as passed.\n\n# Governance\nBLOCKED until the §21.1 owner/team row is filled (merge requirement of PR 14). Re-homed G0 items landing here: DX-04 (causal debugger → G4), DX-06 (neighborhood/mutation → G4, PR 21).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThis is the first demonstration of the product theorem on real code — plan §25's \"brutally concrete\" demo (200-event failure → 4-event Context Pack → repair → receipt) runs on Phase B machinery.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["phase-b"],"parent":"bn-3dl","size":"xl","title":"Phase B — Real-code failure loop"}	blake3:_SjWThrNIjOOQNKuhXERnaQU3pgf25GHSgGSs6Ztn2M
1785275183147263	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwBA		item.create	bn-1fd	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase C (plan §21) makes the loop interactive and trustworthy under reuse: incrementality, editor integrations, corpus at bounded parity, and adoption top-of-funnel. Owns gate G5 (incremental trust). Opening PR: PR 23 (incremental query database) — PR 22a precedes it but is Phase B's ADR deliverable. Contains PRs 15b, 23–27b.\n\n# Goals (plan §21 Phase C deliverables)\n- Incremental semantic database; Incremental Parity Audit (§9.5); LSP/DAP/SARIF adapters.\n- Proof Context Pack format and compiler plumbing (proof-worker efficacy criterion is G6's, evaluated in Phase D).\n- Read-only tokio observation lane: envelopes capped at Observed, every claim dimension Unsupported — adoption top-of-funnel making no controlled-semantics claim.\n- Wave 0/1 corpus (29 families, pinned tlaplus/Examples@91c22ea…) at min(required, P2); P3/P4 raises deferred to Phase D.\n- CML language stability: normalized semantic AST frozen before surface syntax; formatter and migration tool (PR 15b) ship before syntax stability (docs/11 §14).\n- Formative usability sessions (~5 participants per §13.1 persona) each phase from C onward, feeding docs/34 — distinct from and non-binding on the summative G8 study.\n\n# Exit criteria (plan §21)\nEdit/check/explain loop meets the docs/34 latency table (p50/p95, gate-normative for G5, including the explain-interaction row) on the reference workload; incremental results trustworthy under differential audit; reduction engines show zero reachability mismatch on the no-reduction corpus with certified claims falling back to unreduced baseline (docs/08 R04); semantic artifacts byte-identical across the docs/19 determinism matrix. G5 latency is measured with a saturating background swarm present (plan §4.1, docs/34). Evidence queries and context compilation meet docs/34 targets at ≥10^7 evidence nodes.\n\n# Governance\nBLOCKED until the §21.1 row is filled (merge requirement of PR 23) AND the PR 22a incremental-engine ADR exists. Corpus release checkpoint 0.1 closes at Phase C entry.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nInteractive latency and trustworthy reuse are what make the verifier a daily tool instead of a batch oracle; the tokio observed lane is the beachhead for Phase F adoption.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["phase-c"],"parent":"bn-3dl","size":"xl","title":"Phase C — Interactive scale"}	blake3:nfu1VW-H8TETtTMdP56lSth3hapHcjVhR30DJUdt-LI
1785275183257701	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwBQ		item.create	bn-1db	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase D (plan §21) brings the Lean proof service online and extends verification to fairness/liveness, raising corpus parity to full requirements. Owns gate G6 (proof service). Opening PR: PR 28 (Lean proof service). The Lean environment itself was pinned in Phase A (PR 4a) — PR 28 is the service.\n\n# Goals (plan §21 Phase D deliverables)\n- Lean theorem/certificate pipeline; fairness/liveness debugger; invariant/ranking synthesis; proof repair service; refinement receipts.\n- Self-application: a CML model of continuumd's task/continuation lifecycle and INV-017 publication protocol, bound to the daemon implementation via §16 correspondence machinery, checked in CI from Phase D onward; a daemon concurrency defect found this way is recorded as G4-class evidence of product value.\n- Wave 0/1 families raised from the Phase C P2 cap to full required parity (14 at P4, 3 at P3); corpus Waves 2–3 at required parity; corpus checkpoint 0.2 closes in Phase D.\n\n# Exit criteria (plan §21)\nOne nontrivial corpus protocol has safety AND liveness evidence plus real-code refinement, produced by a proof service that holds G6: pinned Lean environment, per-request isolation and cancellation, theorem and axiom manifests on every receipt, agent proof repair accepted only by the kernel.\n\n# Governance\nBLOCKED until the §21.1 row is filled (merge requirement of PR 28). Re-homed G0 items: DX-08 (lens ambiguity → G6), DX-11 (proof-service isolation → G6, PR 28). §8.2 gate 9 (certificate/proof rebuild) enters the promotion profile here.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nProof-backed evidence is the top of the assurance ladder; self-application (Continuum verifying continuumd) is both dogfooding and the strongest possible product argument.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["phase-d"],"parent":"bn-3dl","size":"xl","title":"Phase D — Proof and liveness"}	blake3:irXlYsI9asX4XnVzUvU6R3a9yUPQ-zkCy4FMso0Com4
1785275183368041	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwBg		item.create	bn-4rm	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase E (plan §21) delivers Continuum Forge — synthesis under verification. Owns gate G7. Opening PR: PR 29 (Forge finite CEGIS v0). Forge is deliberately last among the engine phases: it must not be imported by the verifier (START_HERE dependency islands), and it multiplies a loop that must already be trustworthy.\n\n# Goals (plan §21 Phase E deliverables)\n- Typed holes and grammars; CEGIS/interpretation reduction; quality-diversity archive; co-synthesis of algorithm/invariant/ranking/abstraction; materialization to asupersync skeleton.\n- Authoring the expanded docs/48 G8 preregistration (workflows, cohorts, baseline comparator, instruments, pass thresholds) — published before the Phase F summative study (plan §21.1; instruments authored under PR 27b).\n- Corpus checkpoint 0.5 (Waves 0–2 at required parity plus ≥5 named P5 exemplar families in validated-examples.csv) closes in Phase E.\n\n# Exit criteria (plan §21)\nForge rediscovers known solutions through typed holes and finite CEGIS, produces at least one behaviorally novel candidate independently verified within the declared envelope, materializes model/Rust skeleton/proof obligations for a promoted candidate, and reports unrealizability as distinct from unknown.\n\n# Governance\nBLOCKED until the §21.1 row is filled (merge requirement of PR 29). Re-homed G0 item: DX-07 (Forge non-vacuity → G7). Register rows for Forge (research/29, research/30) and explanation science (research/34) must ratify before Phase E opens.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nForge closes the full product loop of plan §25's demonstration — from intent through invention and proof: after the repair demo, Forge must solve the same acknowledgement-guard hole from scratch (ack_guard = synced).\n","kind":"goal","labels":["phase-e"],"parent":"bn-3dl","size":"xl","title":"Phase E — Forge"}	blake3:2lffH8eATncc6jVRnYup8pBHGneHrXTr65c11nhlOpo
1785275183483064	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwBw		item.create	bn-22h	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase F (plan §21) takes Continuum to production evidence, ecosystem adoption, and 1.0. Owns gates G8 (human usability), G9 (corpus interaction parity), G10 (real adoption). Opening PR: deliberately unsequenced — the first PR after PR 30 closes; it MUST be designated in START_HERE before Phase F work begins (there is a child task for this).\n\n# Goals (plan §21 Phase F deliverables)\n- Production partial-order evidence; foreign-runtime instrumentation lane (tokio) upgrading the Phase C read-only lane (instrumentation synthesis, fault-window replay where monitorable, correspondingly bounded envelopes — an adoption bridge, not a claim of controlled semantics).\n- Remote proof/verification workers; multi-agent workbench.\n- All 80 corpus families at declared parity (corpus 1.0, closes with G9); ContinuumBench public release.\n- The preregistered G8 usability study executed and analyzed.\n\n# Exit criteria (plan §21 / G10)\nContinuum replaces bespoke DST plus separate TLA+ workflow in at least two materially different real systems (the two-system criterion is stated identically in §21, §22 G10, and docs/52 G10 — reconciled in one commit per §22's rule). Plus G10's remaining bullets: agent-driven repair used on real changes under review, production evidence returns valid classifications (INV-008), public ContinuumBench, documented assurance envelopes, acceptable operating cost, zero known paths for an unprivileged agent to promote false evidence.\n\n# Scope note (plan §21)\n1.0 verifies systems whose controlled participants live in one workspace snapshot. Cross-repository federation (fed_* manifests) is an explicit 1.0 non-goal — Phase F migrations are recruited within scope.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThis is the payoff phase: G10 is the plan's definition of success (real adoption), not a demo.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["phase-f"],"parent":"bn-3dl","size":"xl","title":"Phase F — Production and ecosystem"}	blake3:VBrnCZWcOHM6FFUd7DRLWR4lDk3yxsiIlRISMOIbjZM
1785275183607574	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwCA		item.create	bn-j09	{"description":"# Context & Background\nEvery HYPOTHESIS-class capability in the plan is owned by a research lane with a baseline, a quantitative promotion threshold, a kill criterion, and a named fallback (plan §24.5). The register is authoritative for lane status; no plan section may claim a lane's output without its status. Today every row is draft or defer — zero rows are ratified.\n\n# What ratification means (mechanical)\nA lane is ratified when its owner fixes the numeric threshold in the research note and the §24.5 register quotes it VERBATIM; both files carry a `quote-id=<slug> \"<text>\"` marker and tools/validate_dossier.py compares the marked quotes for identity (check_register_rows). A row that delegates to a document without quoting is draft by definition. Until ratified, a row blocks its lane's promotion.\n\n# Deadlines (plan §24.5, phase-indexed — wired as bn deps onto each phase's opening PR)\n- Phase A opening: context compilation, ACI, causal minimization.\n- Phase B opening: neighborhood adequacy, workbench security, evidence-graph enforcement.\n- Phase C opening: sub-file incremental trust.\n- Phase D opening: liveness reduction, proof repair, certificate overhead.\n- Phase E opening: Forge co-synthesis+QD, explanation science (via docs/48 preregistration).\n- Phase F opening: production conformance.\n- Rows not named ratify at their lane's opening (exploration reduction, bidirectional lenses, nominal/orbit-finite, sheaf composition).\n- Defer rows are exempt while the deferral stands: weak memory (ADR-0032), timed/probabilistic (ADR-0016), full TLA+ importer (docs/31; ADR-0029) — no bones for these.\n\n# Failure mode to avoid\nA row whose threshold cannot be ratified by its own deadline is a PLAN defect, not a lane defect — it must trigger a plan revision (threshold renegotiation or fallback adoption), never silent slippage.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThis register is the plan's honesty mechanism for research risk: it converts \"we hope X works\" into dated, falsifiable, mechanically-checked commitments with pre-named fallbacks (plan §24 kill criteria are the escape hatches).\n","kind":"goal","labels":["research","governance"],"parent":"bn-3dl","size":"l","title":"Frontier-lane ratification register (plan §24.5)"}	blake3:VE17JOMwy7RANJcvgRF_93tdw7TNZwVbCWrhg2jo8-0
1785275281739212	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwCQ		item.create	bn-2hm	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase A's designated opening PR (plan §21.1) — documentation and decisions, not code. The Revision 3 RFCs started as summaries; review 5 already absorbed large plan sections into RFCs 0026/0027/0030/0032/0037/0038, but full specification form (field types, enums, classification lattices, IDL, versioning, RFC-2119 language) and the remaining validator-tracked spec debt are this PR. Child tasks cover each open debt item (SD-01/07/08/09/10) and the program decisions.\n\n# Why this comes first\nInterface freeze is fail-closed: PR 5 (native protocol kernel) may not merge before PR 0 closes, so every wire-visible decision is settled on paper before any interface exists to ossify. Where plan prose and RFC disagree, the RFC is corrected and becomes NORMATIVE — the plan is a map, not the spec (plan §25).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria (START_HERE PR 0 exit)\n- [ ] RFCs 0026, 0027, 0028, 0030, 0031, 0032, 0037 are normative specifications (field types, enums, lattices, IDL, versioning, RFC-2119 language), each reconciled one-to-one with its plan section.\n- [ ] The pre-freeze open-debt set (plan §25, validator check_spec_debt) reads EMPTY in validation-results.json — not only the seven RFC expansions.\n- [ ] Product license decided (permissive, compatible with asupersync and solver adapters, §21.1).\n- [ ] Corpus per-family redistribution audit exists (required before any public benchmark release).\n- [ ] ADR-0029 rule recorded: foreign oracle tooling (TLC, Apalache, solvers) never ships in release binaries.\n- [ ] Phase A owner/team row filled in START_HERE §21.1 resourcing table (merge requirement; clears program_status=blocked for Phase A).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nEvery fail-closed property later phases rely on (typed errors, epochs, authority splits, redaction) is specified here before code exists to violate it.\n","kind":"goal","labels":["phase-a","docs","governance"],"parent":"bn-19p","size":"l","title":"PR 0 — Specification pass and program decisions [G1, G2]"}	blake3:eT5SPwAfurVkGsZSsqRgz4jfJVu8BcSZGDYbmaZaNrU
1785275281866181	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwCg		item.create	bn-djj	{"description":"# Context & Background\nFirst code PR: the Rust workspace skeleton and the constitutional invariants (plan §2, §20) that everything else compiles against. START_HERE PR 1.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Rust workspace and crate boundaries per plan §20 (no continuum-protocol crate — the native protocol lives in continuumd; certificate checker may not depend on search; model core may not depend on asupersync; adapters may not own semantic state; Forge may not be imported by the verifier).\n- Protocol, semantic, intent, evidence, proof, and corpus epochs as first-class types.\n- Assurance and inconclusive enums; claim-status lattice.\n- #![forbid(unsafe_code)] defaults; deterministic build and artifact paths.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] An unsupported empty task returns a valid machine result naming every epoch and no misleading success flag (START_HERE PR 1 exit).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nEpochs and the claim-status lattice are the vocabulary of honesty for every artifact the system will ever emit; getting them into types first makes misleading results unrepresentable.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","kernel"],"parent":"bn-19p","size":"m","title":"PR 1 — Revision 3 constitution and epochs [G1]"}	blake3:tDg54nRxyrEw1YXH3EqQGuqIBU9gSibQzNNwU0G09tg
1785275281977743	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwCw		item.create	bn-2y0	{"description":"# Context & Background\nContent addressing under concurrency is freeze-blocking G0 question DX-13 (\"Does content addressing survive concurrency?\", G0_SPIKE_MATRIX). This PR closes it. START_HERE PR 2.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Exact finite values from Revision 2; canonical encoding and total order.\n- Content identity per ADR-0013: in certified lanes, canonical content identity is primary and hash collisions are resolved by canonical comparison; 256-bit-hash identity only in explicitly labeled non-certified modes.\n- Collision-injection tests; atomic artifact publication; authorization separate from handle possession.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Concurrent publication of identical artifacts yields one identity (no lost receipts, deterministic result — DX-13 pass condition).\n- [ ] Artificial hash collisions are detected and resolved by canonical comparison in certified lanes.\n- [ ] G0_SPIKE_MATRIX DX-13 row flipped from \"Open — freeze-blocking\" to evidence, and plan §0.3 derived counts updated (validator g0_matrix_counts enforces agreement).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nCAS identity is the root of every receipt, snapshot, and evidence node; a collision or lost-publication bug here poisons all downstream trust. Failure consequence per the matrix: redesign CAS/task transaction.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","kernel","g0"],"parent":"bn-19p","size":"l","title":"PR 2 — Canonical values and CAS primitives [G0 (DX-13), G1]"}	blake3:C7cFz94Q_rDhFamU00k07lVTbI86szCUdntZOsrsMrI
1785275282102468	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwDA		item.create	bn-2zm	{"description":"# Context & Background\nImmutable Merkle workspace snapshots — the \"immutable snapshot\" step of the product loop. START_HERE PR 3; schema already exists (schemas/workspace-snapshot.schema.json, ten §4.2 components, protocol epoch deliberately excluded from identity per §4.6).\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Merkle workspace snapshots; disk import, editor overlay, fork, seal, diff.\n- Source/dependency/toolchain/config identities; stale-snapshot error; deterministic file ordering.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Two clients can fork and analyze independently.\n- [ ] An old snapshot remains reproducible after the working tree changes.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nAll verification is pinned to snapshot identity; stale-state rejection (G1/G2) starts here.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","kernel"],"parent":"bn-19p","size":"m","title":"PR 3 — Workspace snapshots [G1]"}	blake3:vz3XvSZpcf_GucdNm5cXqP8T4-Z8wDm1yPj86vP36J0
1785275282230274	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwDQ		item.create	bn-37d	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe protected Intent Contract (plan §5) — the artifact the whole anti-reward-hacking design defends. START_HERE PR 4. Schema exists (schemas/intent-contract.schema.json) with fail-closed field-level change policy; SD-07 (structured property-AST expression form) is paid under PR 0 before this freezes.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nSchema/types for: properties; assumptions; observers; bounds; faults; fairness; assurance policy; optimization/non-vacuity; field-level change policy.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Die Hard and replicated-register intents serialize canonically.\n- [ ] Ordinary operations cannot mutate a protected intent (mutation requires the privileged intent.propose_revision/accept path, RFC 0037).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nG3 (intent integrity) and G0-DX-02 (formal reward hacking detection) are only meaningful against a canonical, protected intent representation.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","intent"],"parent":"bn-19p","size":"m","title":"PR 4 — Intent Contract v0 [G1, G3]"}	blake3:udPggdXaHP5CsmiUr6G2bWqDyoVx38Xb4p59CEPeu1Q
1785275282350279	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwDg		item.create	bn-3af	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPulled forward from Phase D by design (plan §21 pulls the Lean pinning and foundational theorems into Phase A so the proof lane's toolchain risk is retired early). START_HERE PR 4a. The proof SERVICE remains PR 28.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Pin Lean toolchain leanprover/lean4:v4.32.1.\n- Kernel-check the existing seed modules under lean/Continuum/ (17 .lean files exist in the dossier; never yet elaborated — the validator only scans for placeholders, see VALIDATION_REPORT Lean boundary).\n- T0/T1 theorems: transition-system safety, stuttering simulation, finite-closure certificate soundness.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] T0/T1 compile with no `sorry` and empty axiom manifests (RFC 0012 theorem ladder; axiom manifests per ADR-0035).\n- [ ] lake build green in CI under the pinned toolchain.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nRetires the \"do our Lean sources even elaborate?\" risk that the dossier explicitly flags as unvalidated, years before G6 needs the full service.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","proof"],"parent":"bn-19p","size":"m","title":"PR 4a — Lean environment and seed theorems [G6; Phase A band]"}	blake3:5F_xvBl9x43kVO2WePQOw_p9_vWc7oKwLLZfs2JH-6o
1785275282463202	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwDw		item.create	bn-yx2	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe agent-native protocol (plan §10) — request/response types and local transport inside continuumd. START_HERE PR 5. THIS IS THE FREEZE POINT: PR 0's spec debt must be empty before any interface here ossifies (wired as a bn dep).\n\n# Implementation Approach\nOperations per plan §10.2: workspace.create/fork/diff/seal; intent.get/diff/propose_revision/accept/reject/lock; verification.start; task.status/cancel/resume/subscribe; task.update_budget; evidence.get/query/verify; evidence.subscribe; capability negotiation; typed errors (§10.3 full error-code set incl. AcceptanceChainInvalid, StatusConflict, QuotaExhausted, EpochUnsupported, PublicationAborted) and idempotency keys. Protocol-major version window: N and N−1 (RFC 0026).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Replaying an idempotent request returns the same task/artifact identity.\n- [ ] May not merge before PR 0 closes (dep enforced in this graph).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nB2 (ACI as a first-class surface) is a core design bet; every agent, CLI, and adapter interaction flows through these types.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","protocol"],"parent":"bn-19p","size":"l","title":"PR 5 — Native protocol kernel [G1, G2]"}	blake3:-w7HE_BsTzoXGGxzqJzDWM6DU7-1iFq9mP_eWiyIpJ4
1785275282595466	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwEA		item.create	bn-19u	{"description":"# Context & Background\nCloses freeze-blocking G0-DX-14 (\"Can cancellation of verification work close correctly?\"). Uses asupersync regions for daemon work — the runtime's own structured-cancellation discipline applied to the verifier. START_HERE PR 6.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nTask lifecycle; committed partial evidence; budget accounting; suspension/continuation; drain/finalize behavior; no orphan workers. verification-task schema already requires a continuation on `suspended` and on BudgetExhausted (typed exception for genuinely non-resumable exhaustion — SD-13, paid).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Cancellation at every instrumented phase leaves either a valid continuation or no published partial artifact (DX-14 pass condition: no leaked obligations; resumable artifacts either committed or absent).\n- [ ] G0_SPIKE_MATRIX DX-14 row flipped to evidence; §0.3 counts updated (validator-enforced).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nFailure consequence per the matrix: integrate deeper with asupersync. Cancel-correctness is what lets budget-bounded agents drive long verification safely (B19, research/09 cancellation calculus lane).\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","kernel","g0"],"parent":"bn-19p","size":"l","title":"PR 6 — Cancel-correct task service [G0 (DX-14), G1]"}	blake3:__R8eVWBLh_omPX5QTVTOD6Fgx5nRAeofqhDGHwK64U
1785275282723719	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwEQ		item.create	bn-20s	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe multi-agent Evidence Graph (plan §11) in single-writer v0 form. START_HERE PR 7. Schemas exist (evidence-graph-node/edge) with fail-closed conditionals: service_identity required on Sampled/Bounded/Validated/Proved promotion; CHECKED_BY edges require checker (INV-004) — SD-11, paid.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nNode/edge/status types, immutable versions, queries; support/refute/depend/refine/explain/repair/check edges; provenance; trusted status transitions; conflict nodes.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] An untrusted client cannot promote a proposal to validated/proved (authority separated from handle possession).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nG0-DX-12 (intent stability across swarm work) validated the authority TABLE in a finite spike; this PR is the real single-writer implementation. Full concurrent enforcement is the §11.7/RFC 0038 register row (Phase B deadline) — deliberately not claimed here (docs/53 boundary).\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","evidence"],"parent":"bn-19p","size":"m","title":"PR 7 — Evidence Graph v0 [G1]"}	blake3:_jg5IyTwEbO56k0ucoBGPbZC9LVLBM9rjE8yNhC6ma8
1785275282843449	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwEg		item.create	bn-2ge	{"description":"# Context & Background\nWraps the executable Revision 2 reference semantics behind the native protocol — the first engine behind the daemon. START_HERE PR 8.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nProgrammatic transition model; deterministic BFS; invariant/deadlock checking; shortest witness; finite closure certificate.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Die Hard returns 16 states and depth-6 solution through the daemon API (matching the Revision 2 reference results).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nGives PRs 10/11 (ACI benchmark, Context Packs) something real to run against, and produces the certificates PR 9's kernel checks.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","engine"],"parent":"bn-19p","size":"m","title":"PR 8 — Exact finite model service [G1, G2]"}	blake3:gNQPjm6asOCuU7XLS6rnPpnOYPUhyrz1F5qR5mKlBSA
1785275282959180	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwEw		item.create	bn-6tv	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe trusted checking base — the only code whose correctness the whole evidence architecture ultimately rests on. Phase-normative covenant (plan §21 Phase A, docs/03), not just a PR annotation. START_HERE PR 9.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nFour crates: continuum-kernel-core, continuum-kernel-sat, continuum-kernel-smt, continuum-kernel-temporal (plan §20).\n- Finite closure/type certificate checking.\n- No shared evaluator code with any engine; no async, no unsafe, no plugins or dynamic loading.\n- <15,000 non-test-line covenant across the four crates (docs/03).\n- Serialization boundary: certificates are checked from wire form, never from shared memory.\n- Receipt generation with checker epoch, build digest, and input hashes.\n- Mutation tests.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every single-field certificate mutation in the test suite is rejected, checking wire-form input only.\n- [ ] Line covenant enforced in CI.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Agent proof repair accepted only by kernel\" (G6) and \"certificate mutations rejected\" only mean something if the kernel is small, isolated, and independently auditable. This is the trust anchor.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","kernel"],"parent":"bn-19p","size":"l","title":"PR 9 — Trusted kernel crates [G1, G6]"}	blake3:WUDbBJBF-xyBLLCCsD6eAWYFgdn2N783g8Yk-1oydE4
1785275283083241	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwFA		item.create	bn-38i	{"description":"# Context & Background\nCloses freeze-blocking G0-DX-10 (\"Can agents use the protocol more effectively than CLI scraping?\") — the plan's willingness to falsify its own central bet (B2). START_HERE PR 10.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nMinimal client exposing only typed operations; compare against a disciplined shell-scraping baseline on Die Hard and Dining Philosophers tasks. Measure: valid operation rate; tokens/bytes; task completion; recovery from errors; deterministic reproduction. Harness design per research/25 (ACI register row — must be ratified at Phase A opening; wired as dep on PR 0).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Native ACI is measurably more effective (success and cost) than the disciplined shell baseline, OR the protocol is redesigned before freeze — a redesign outcome is a VALID exit, not a failure to hide.\n- [ ] The Phase A benchmark subset passes the plan §19.4 family/source-hash separation check before the result is accepted (G0 staging rule; full leakage validation stays DX-15 at G9).\n- [ ] Matrix DX-10 row flipped; §0.3 counts updated.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nIf typed surfaces lose to shell scraping, the ACI-centric architecture is wrong and must change before freeze — this is exactly what G0 exists to catch.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","aci","g0"],"parent":"bn-19p","size":"m","title":"PR 10 — Agent client and ACI benchmark harness [G0 (DX-10), G2]"}	blake3:m01uRC31RyMUdEFyRqCQFnlZnInA1AfekoAPmK4_POA
1785275283213015	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwFQ		item.create	bn-8mx	{"description":"# Context & Background\nContext Packs (plan §6) — bounded, replay-preserving failure artifacts for agents; the alternative to raw-log dumping validated by G0-DX-01's spike (200-event failure → 4-event core). START_HERE PR 11. Schema exists (context-pack.schema.json, mirrors the canonical nine-dimension assurance envelope, identity-checked by the validator — SD-12, paid).\n\n# Implementation Approach\nTarget/verdict/assurance; state and event slice; source/model references; omissions and expansion handles; replay reference; byte/token budgets. Start with graph reachability and invariant failures.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The synthetic 200-event durability case compiles to a replay-preserving core with substantial reduction (spike parity).\n- [ ] Packs carry omission manifests and expansion handles (G2 bullet).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe spike validated the artifact SHAPE only (196 noise events were semantically inert — plan §25 boundary). This PR builds the real compiler; the general-compilation win margin is the research/25 register row.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","aci"],"parent":"bn-19p","size":"m","title":"PR 11 — Context Pack schema and compiler v0 [G2]"}	blake3:gTuILHtTXNNpW3Cmk1yejq_vpaq-l7nhP6TXjEZEsG8
1785275283335690	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwFg		item.create	bn-1r9	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe reward-hacking detector (plan §5.3, G0-DX-02): classifies intent changes as privileged across all seven diff dimensions. START_HERE PR 12. Schema exists (semantic-diff.schema.json; `protected` is structurally bound const:true — SD-11, paid).\n\n# Implementation Approach\nClassify supported changes: exact equality; property AST edit; assumption add/remove; bound change; observer event change; fault/fairness/assurance change. Solver-based implication only where sound and bounded. Changes outside supported fragments classify as Unknown and BLOCK ordinary promotion (fail-closed, G3 bullet) rather than passing silently.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All G0 intent-gaming patches classify as privileged changes; a source-only guard repair does not.\n- [ ] Unsupported-fragment changes yield Unknown + blocked promotion, never silent pass.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThis is the enforcement point for \"no ordinary repair promotes with a protected-intent change\" — the single most load-bearing anti-gaming property in the plan.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","intent"],"parent":"bn-19p","size":"m","title":"PR 12 — Intent semantic diff v0 [G3]"}	blake3:gaKQq36d5szfRXoZmbYyCL_3lxAZY4weuhUHGX6RC6g
1785275283463249	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwFw		item.create	bn-1xn	{"description":"# Context & Background\nStable human commands over the same daemon surface agents use — prose is a projection of the JSON, never a separate truth (plan §13.4/§3.1). START_HERE PR 13.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nCommands: continuum snapshot / check / explain / debug / repair / evidence show / context expand / task status|resume|cancel. Support --json; golden tests.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Golden tests pin JSON output, exit codes, and concise terminal output.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nFirst-party dogfooding surface for the implementation team (the plan's declared first users), and the substrate for the G8 accessibility bullet (no critical workflow requires color or a rendered graph).\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","ux"],"parent":"bn-19p","size":"m","title":"PR 13 — Human CLI v0 [G1]"}	blake3:gp7JnTaDdB9lEkHdIsZu926mviXF32pwHrWgzZJou48
1785275401654078	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwGA		item.create	bn-1bf	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe seven load-bearing Revision 3 RFCs (plan §25) must go from summary+absorbed-prose form to full specifications. Review 5 already absorbed the missing plan content (RFC 0026: version window/budget updates/evidence subscriptions + five §10.3 error codes; 0030: auditability classes + derived sampling rate; 0032: cost governance + canonical gate enum; 0037: intent bundles and convergence; 0038: write/concurrency model) — what remains is SPECIFICATION FORM.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nPer RFC: field types, enums, classification lattices, versioning rules, RFC-2119 MUST/SHOULD/MAY language; then reconcile one-to-one with its plan section. Where they disagree, correct the RFC and make it NORMATIVE (the plan is a map, not the spec — plan §25). Note: this task is sized l but is one homogeneous editing pass over seven documents with an identical checklist per document; splitting it into seven bones would add bookkeeping, not information.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each of the seven RFCs contains typed field definitions and RFC-2119 normative language for every operation/artifact it owns.\n- [ ] One-to-one reconciliation with plan §4/§8/§9/§10/§11 sections recorded in each RFC's header.\n- [ ] Dossier validator remains green (retired-name, gate-citation, and link checks all pass on the edited files).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nPR 5 freezes interfaces against these specs; ambiguity surviving past freeze becomes permanent wire debt.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","docs"],"parent":"bn-2hm","size":"l","title":"Expand RFCs 0026/0027/0028/0030/0031/0032/0037 to normative specification form"}	blake3:470-9t_Yku7zP81zwPDjnKQDpYxnGvSMdkAuk8iepk8
1785275401789088	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwGQ		item.create	bn-1cw	{"description":"# Context & Background\nValidator-tracked spec debt SD-01 (plan §25, check_spec_debt): RFC 0026 references a normative IDL file for the native protocol that does not exist yet.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nAuthor the IDL covering the full §10.2 operation set (workspace.*, intent.*, verification.start, task.* incl. update_budget, evidence.* incl. subscribe, capability negotiation) with the §10.3 typed error codes; reference it from RFC 0026; teach the validator's sd01 predicate to verify its presence/shape so the ledger flips to paid mechanically.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] IDL file exists and covers every §10.2 operation name.\n- [ ] check_spec_debt reports SD-01 paid; plan §25 marker updated in the same commit (the ledger is edited only together with its predicates).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nGenerated clients and schemas for the native protocol are a G2 bullet; an IDL is their single source.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","docs","spec-debt"],"parent":"bn-2hm","size":"s","title":"SD-01 — Author RFC 0026's normative IDL file"}	blake3:udA8YjwYxtaDMtqt_F_onZajcrrbHG2n2JcX2rBzsxM
1785275401913567	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwGg		item.create	bn-1lz	{"description":"# Context & Background\nSpec debt SD-07 (plan §25): schemas/intent-contract.schema.json currently carries a bare `expression` string for properties. The semantic diff (PR 12) needs a structured AST with canonical normalization to classify property edits soundly — a string diff cannot distinguish refactoring from weakening.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nReplace the bare string with a structured property-AST expression form (canonical normalization defined; the AST is what §5's diff dimensions operate over). Update example instances; keep the schema fail-closed. Coordinate with PR 4 (Intent Contract v0 types) — this lands first, in PR 0.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] intent-contract.schema.json expresses properties as a structured AST with a canonical normal form.\n- [ ] All schema/example pairs still validate; check_spec_debt reports SD-07 paid.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nG3's \"property AST edit\" diff dimension and DX-02's reward-hacking detection are only sound over a canonical AST.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","intent","spec-debt"],"parent":"bn-2hm","size":"m","title":"SD-07 — Structured property-AST expression form in intent-contract schema"}	blake3:lBV5vUeqMHKozsZsWReAVHo15Ck4_kABy-CqL4AQJV0
1785275402037661	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwGw		item.create	bn-xd2	{"description":"# Context & Background\nSpec debt SD-08 (plan §25): the 20 dossier schemas use three rival $id/versioning conventions and zero schema-epoch fields, but §4.3 requires epoch-carrying artifacts.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nPick one $id/versioning convention; add a schema-epoch field to every schema; update all example instances; extend the sd08 predicate to enforce the convention mechanically.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All schemas share one $id/versioning convention and carry a schema-epoch field.\n- [ ] check_spec_debt reports SD-08 paid; all 19 schema/example pairs validate.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nTwo-epoch migration (§4.6) and Preserved|Revalidate|Incompatible compatibility statements need a machine-readable schema epoch to exist at all.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","docs","spec-debt"],"parent":"bn-2hm","size":"m","title":"SD-08 — One $id/versioning convention with schema-epoch field across all schemas"}	blake3:z6Aai4xVcxpYiI9lyJwG51_qXuYsZ7fDjy9QTwcpAVI
1785275402152877	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwHA		item.create	bn-v1c	{"description":"# Context & Background\nSpec debt SD-09 (plan §25): the operational contract lives only in plan prose. The owning documents must absorb: purge and Redacted(reason, commitment) semantics (§4.5); backup and verified restore; the cross-user dedup existence-oracle rule (a privacy leak if unspecified); two-epoch migration; Preserved|Revalidate|Incompatible compatibility statements; engine-defect artifacts (§4.7).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] docs/35, RFC 0026, ADR-0018, and docs/42 each carry their slice of the operational contract with RFC-2119 language.\n- [ ] check_spec_debt reports SD-09 paid.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nOperational honesty (what purge really deletes, what restore really verifies) is part of the same fail-closed doctrine as the gates; leaving it in plan prose means it never becomes implementation requirements.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","docs","spec-debt"],"parent":"bn-2hm","size":"m","title":"SD-09 — Absorb §4.5/§4.6/§4.7 operational contract into docs/35, RFC 0026, ADR-0018, docs/42"}	blake3:mnnJxEYZ7xj-HAGxKp9BUZJEM6kqau_c25gpfWVZkJo
1785275402244320	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwHQ		item.create	bn-7kj	{"description":"# Context & Background\nSpec debt SD-10 (plan §25): docs/41 predates the 12-gate promotion design and the phase-staged gate profiles; RFC 0032 is normative in the interim. The regenerated document must carry the marker `<!-- regenerated: rfc-0032 -->` (the sd10 predicate looks for it).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] docs/41 regenerated: 12 named gates, phase-profile staging (gates 1–8, 11–12 Phase B; 10 Phase C; 9 Phase D), NotYetEnforced semantics.\n- [ ] check_spec_debt reports SD-10 paid.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nRemoves the last stale-document hazard in the promotion path before PR 20/22 implement it.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","docs","spec-debt"],"parent":"bn-2hm","size":"s","title":"SD-10 — Regenerate docs/41 around the 12-gate, phase-profile repair design"}	blake3:6c-GDZCE7Zq5Xcj61Ngw9hf2jxX7yZmkJqyRxmMumjE
1785275402359306	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwHg		item.create	bn-2br	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThree Phase A program decisions bundled into PR 0 (plan §21.1): (1) product license — permissive, compatible with asupersync and solver adapters; (2) the ADR-0029 rule that foreign oracle tooling (TLC, Apalache, solvers) never ships in release binaries; (3) a per-family redistribution audit of the TLA+ corpus (80 validated + extended rows, corpus/tla-examples/) — REQUIRED before any public benchmark release (wired as a dep onto the Phase F ContinuumBench release bone).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] License chosen and recorded (LICENSE + ADR).\n- [ ] ADR-0029 rule stated normatively.\n- [ ] Redistribution audit: per-family license/provenance table for the pinned tlaplus/Examples commit.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nLegal/ecosystem groundwork whose absence would block the public releases in Phase F; cheap now, expensive under deadline.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","governance"],"parent":"bn-2hm","size":"s","title":"Program decisions: product license, ADR-0029 oracle-shipping rule, corpus redistribution audit"}	blake3:Tgv3D5YrRIbocHFYSsChgP_4oiMCTZZWJZfafd01Qzk
1785275402482185	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwHw		item.create	bn-1ng	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe §21.1 resourcing table in notes/START_HERE_IMPLEMENTATION.md has every owner/minimum-viable-team row unassigned, so the validator derives program_status=blocked for all six phases (check_program_status; surfaced in validation-results.json and VALIDATION_REPORT.md). Filling a phase's row is a merge requirement of that phase's opening PR — for Phase A, PR 0.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Phase A row names an owner and minimum viable team.\n- [ ] Validator-derived program status for Phase A is no longer BLOCKED; plan §0.3's Program-status bullet stays consistent (validator-enforced).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nPhases are gate-driven, not time-driven — but a phase without an owner is BLOCKED, not in progress. This is the plan's own anti-vaporware rule; it applies to us first.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-a","governance"],"parent":"bn-2hm","size":"xs","title":"Fill Phase A owner/team resourcing row (§21.1)"}	blake3:065Imh436JtwwcXXDZdqE9PtEiDiL-I-fjEo3nhELdc
1785275402609936	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwIA		item.create	bn-2en	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase B's opening PR (plan §21.1): instrument asupersync's narrow primitives to emit canonical semantic events — the raw material for CIR, minimization, and the debugger. START_HERE PR 14.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nJournal task/region lifecycle; reserve/commit/abort; cancellation phases; obligations; virtual time; channel communication. One adapter crate; supported upstream window N and N−1 covered by the adapter conformance corpus in CI against upstream pre-releases; an upstream breaking release opens a scheduled adapter epoch (§4.6) visible in envelopes; explicit semantic-hook contract; the model core never depends on asupersync (dependency-island rule).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Identical controlled choice logs produce canonical identical semantic events.\n- [ ] Phase B owner/team row filled (merge requirement of this PR, §21.1).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nEverything G4 promises (causal debugging of REAL code) starts from a trustworthy semantic journal; canonicality here is what makes replay exact.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-b","adapter"],"parent":"bn-3zt","size":"m","title":"PR 14 — Asupersync semantic journal [G4]"}	blake3:boMiT3EdOb53rxjrBfsfibTvPcyraZ_plfcyO5dmPvg
1785275402722969	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwIQ		item.create	bn-1nn	{"description":"# Context & Background\nEnvironment-effect packs — only the profiles the replicated register needs (deliberate minimalism; START_HERE PR 15).\n\n# Implementation Approach\nDelivery/drop/duplicate/delay/partition; crash/restart/epoch; submit/stable/sync/ack; declared unsupported cases (unsupported is a valid, explicit answer — never silent).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Crash windows and cancellation points replay exactly.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nFault packs are what turn the register from a toy into a distributed-systems test article; declared-unsupported honesty feeds the assurance envelope.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-b","adapter"],"parent":"bn-3zt","size":"m","title":"PR 15 — Network/process/storage packs [G4]"}	blake3:TCysMiUE3P2rWI4mU7Nl99P-9VoTjkh9C0M4pVHYz0s
1785275402842622	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwIg		item.create	bn-3gy	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe Continuum Modeling Language lands as a Finite core fragment ONLY (START_HERE governing rule; full surface language is deliberately deferred until after PR 30). START_HERE PR 15a.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nCrates continuum-cml-syntax and continuum-cml-elab; the Finite fragment; enough surface for the replicated register and the Wave 0 corpus ports. The programmatic model API remains supported — CML is a second front end, not a replacement.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The replicated-register model written in CML elaborates to the same semantic model identity as its programmatic equivalent (identity, not just equivalence — content-addressed).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nSame-identity elaboration is the proof that surface syntax adds zero semantic risk; it also unblocks the Wave 0 CML ports (PR 27a) and the formatter (PR 15b).\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-b","cml"],"parent":"bn-3zt","size":"l","title":"PR 15a — CML core-fragment parser and elaborator [G4]"}	blake3:Gt_59Y3jcXSjcO6_4tnvPZxKuj2bxkoQg-XlI9w5MLw
1785275402958213	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwIw		item.create	bn-oqc	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe program's canonical real-code article: an abstract atomic register, an operational durable register, and a real asupersync implementation, plus the mutant family every later gate exercises. START_HERE PR 16.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nAbstract atomic register; operational durable register; asupersync implementation; correct version; ack-before-sync, lost-abort, stale-epoch, and orphan mutants.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each mutant has an expected intent/property and deterministic campaign.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nAck-before-durable is the plan §25 demonstration failure; this PR manufactures it (and its cousins) reproducibly. The mutants also seed the development gaming suite (§19.4).\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-b","engine"],"parent":"bn-3zt","size":"m","title":"PR 16 — Replicated-register model and implementation [G4]"}	blake3:Y5tXO0oZlj7upi87pzq3qlLXg8SHhBgCCVLxAUKZ24c
1785275403079596	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwJA		item.create	bn-2cz	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe Common Interaction Representation and §16 correspondence machinery: how concrete asupersync executions project onto abstract model states. START_HERE PR 17.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nEvent/configuration validation; causal/conflict edges; concrete/abstract state projection; stuttering classification; uncovered semantic effect errors (an unmapped effect is an ERROR, not a silent gap — fail-closed).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The correct implementation satisfies bounded refinement.\n- [ ] Mutants fail at mapped transitions (the failure points to the right abstract transition).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nCorrespondence is what lets model-level evidence speak about real code — the load-bearing claim of the whole product (§16); also the substrate for Phase D refinement receipts and the continuumd self-application.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-b","engine"],"parent":"bn-3zt","size":"l","title":"PR 17 — CIR and concrete/abstract correspondence [G4]"}	blake3:8W_4Ro3UnPnDwwFGZb62G_h_ybMlecvnbhEcT1wr1ec
1785275403202252	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwJQ		item.create	bn-8pr	{"description":"# Context & Background\nReduces failing traces to replay-preserving causal cores — the engine behind Context Packs' headline reduction (G0-DX-01 validated the artifact shape on a synthetic trace; this is the real engine). START_HERE PR 18. Register row: causal minimization (research/26, ≤10% of trace length on real non-synthetic failures — ratifies at Phase A opening).\n\n# Implementation Approach\nDeletion, causal-closure, owner/fault/value reduction, and replay validation.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Ack-before-sync failure reduces to a compact core.\n- [ ] Reduction does NOT delete the actual causal mechanism (replay-preservation check).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nKill criterion (research/26): if minimization cost dominates verification, fall back to 1-minimal delta debugging only. Cheap, correct cores are what make agent diagnosis affordable.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-b","debugger"],"parent":"bn-3zt","size":"m","title":"PR 18 — Causal minimization [G4]"}	blake3:vkUTyCAfXK2Qv2M2MqIwmJdHscr6vsIlqMA0oeyL7zQ
1785275403321291	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwJg		item.create	bn-1be	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe partial-order causal debugger (plan §7) — closes re-homed G0-DX-04 (branch before the Ack/Sync race must reproduce expected safe/failing outcomes; artifact shape came from the Phase A spike, engine evidence runs here per plan §22). START_HERE PR 19.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nSelected configuration; enabled frontier; semantic step/reverse; branch on alternate event; state/observer/obligation views; why-enabled/blocked derivations.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Branch before Sync/Ack shows safe and failing successors from one handle (DX-04 pass condition, now at G4).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nAlternate-branch exploration is the G4 bullet that distinguishes causal debugging from log reading; DAP (PR 26) projects this core.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-b","debugger"],"parent":"bn-3zt","size":"l","title":"PR 19 — Verification debugger core [G4]"}	blake3:BWtdIoxBqnSpUgvtry1peMEe_CCKsOtmAsbJ4g1uTnw
1785275403458088	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwJw		item.create	bn-2ft	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe repair transaction (plan §8): the auditable unit in which an agent proposes, evaluates, and promotes a fix. START_HERE PR 20. Schema exists (repair-transaction.schema.json: 12-gate identity list, phase-profile conditionals, no not_applicable — SD-11, paid).\n\n# Implementation Approach\nBegin/apply/evaluate/promote with: hypothesis; patch identity; exact replay; semantic/intent diff; evidence accumulation; policy verdict.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Moving ack after sync is evaluable end-to-end.\n- [ ] A property-weakening patch is reclassified as an intent change and BLOCKED (G3 integration).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThis is the loop's transactional spine — where diagnosis becomes an accountable change instead of a hopeful patch.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-b","repair"],"parent":"bn-3zt","size":"m","title":"PR 20 — Repair Transaction v0 [G3, G4]"}	blake3:RHV8_h6OfhsuhUESdgPf23eR5_sij1ufbMCJUcU_DKs
1785275403566889	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwKA		item.create	bn-2gm	{"description":"# Context & Background\nAnti-overfitting for repairs: a patch that fixes only the exact trace must fail (G0-DX-06, re-homed to G4/PR 21 in the matrix). START_HERE PR 21. Register row: neighborhood adequacy (research/33, lane to be opened; hidden-variant catch rate on the docs/50-classified gaming corpus; Phase B ratification deadline).\n\n# Implementation Approach\nGenerate semantic neighbors around the causal core; run known property/model mutants; grade under the bounded envelope.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A hard-coded exact-trace repair fails the campaign.\n- [ ] The semantic guard repair passes the bounded envelope.\n- [ ] G0 matrix DX-06 row updated with evidence.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nWithout this, \"the exact replay passes\" is gameable by memorizing the trace; the neighborhood is what makes promotion mean something. Fallback if adequacy can't ratify: fixed strategy-list neighborhood with per-receipt coverage disclosure and NO adequacy claim.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-b","repair","g0"],"parent":"bn-3zt","size":"m","title":"PR 21 — Neighboring exploration and mutation challenge [G4; closes re-homed G0-DX-06]"}	blake3:dP_eAdI3gzfhVu7IO-3AvazUZZ3gCzmkvB90AEcUT7Q
1785275403685348	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwKQ		item.create	bn-jxh	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe promotion receipt (plan §8.6): the composed, independently verifiable record of why a change was allowed. START_HERE PR 22. Schema exists (promotion-receipt.schema.json: gate profile, cost ledger, INV-014 checker identity — SD-11/12, paid).\n\n# Implementation Approach\nCompose: intent identity; before/after snapshots; semantic diff; replay/neighborhood/mutation results; refinement/certificate status; unknowns; gate_profile (the phase-staged profile evaluated under, plan §21); NotYetEnforced list — gates not yet in the profile, NEVER rendered as passed; policy decision.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Receipt independently verifies references (every hash/handle resolves and matches).\n- [ ] Receipt cannot be forged by the agent client (authority check, not schema check).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nA Phase B receipt is structurally distinguishable from a Phase D receipt — the honesty mechanism that lets the program ship staged trust without lying about it.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-b","evidence"],"parent":"bn-3zt","size":"m","title":"PR 22 — Promotion receipts [G3, G4]"}	blake3:VEo3SAt3OmWcQVSt2lOwdSkoVaZaWJTLT-feKEmBtG0
1785275403809662	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwKg		item.create	bn-20w	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase C is BLOCKED until this ADR exists (plan §21, docs/08 R21) — the build-vs-adopt decision for the §9 incremental engine (salsa-derived vs custom) must be evidence-backed, not vibes-backed. START_HERE PR 22a; delivered in Phase B, unblocks PR 23.\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- ADR resolving build-vs-adopt with explicit criteria.\n- Spike implementing the four reuse-edge classes (Exact/Validated/Conservative/Experimental) and query.explain_invalidation over parse/elaborate/explore.\n- A measured invalidation-precision baseline recorded in the ADR.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] ADR merged with spike evidence and the invalidation-precision baseline.\n- [ ] PR 23 dep satisfied (wired in this graph).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nR21 (docs/08) flags the incremental engine as a top program risk; the kill signal is Conservative-collapse (reuse degrading to always-conservative). The baseline measured here is what G5's audit later regresses against.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-b","incremental"],"parent":"bn-3zt","size":"m","title":"PR 22a — Incremental-engine ADR and reuse-edge spike [G5]"}	blake3:1gAU8q3--VJOEBZ8fvl0zwRwITtVks-IuRak22ccHB0
1785275403929242	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwKw		item.create	bn-1f6	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPlan §19.4 splits gaming suites: a DEVELOPMENT suite (used for G3, grown freely) and a HELD-OUT suite (evaluated once, at G9). The development corpus, classified by docs/50 attack class (≥N mutations per class, N fixed at the research/33 lane opening), is a Phase B G3 deliverable — §24.5's neighborhood-adequacy row explicitly notes no such corpus exists today. Sources include engine defects (§19.4 engine-defect source added in review 5).\n\n# Implementation Approach\nBuild the corpus from: PR 16 mutants, §19.5 reward-hacking mutation list (property weakening, assumption strengthening, bound reduction, observer hiding, fault removal, lower-assurance substitution, vacuity injection, instrumentation tampering, resource attacks, distractor edits), and docs/50 attack taxonomy. Keep held-out variants sealed under §19.4 family/source-hash separation.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every docs/50 attack class has ≥N classified development mutations.\n- [ ] G3's development-suite bullet is satisfiable: every supported-fragment mutation classifies as privileged intent change across all seven diff dimensions.\n- [ ] Held-out suite remains unexamined (leakage discipline, DX-15/G9).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nG3 without a serious adversarial corpus is a rubber stamp; this corpus is the whetstone for the intent-integrity claim, and its held-out sibling is how G9 proves we didn't train to the test.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-b","intent","security"],"parent":"bn-3zt","size":"m","title":"Development gaming-mutation corpus (docs/50-classified) [G3]"}	blake3:cwizSwea3MadFI9k-iBIIOBQ-RuPG4Fw3zXEDvwkxIY
1785275510605280	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwLA		item.create	bn-380	{"description":"# Context & Background\nCML language stability discipline (plan §21 Phase C, docs/11 §14): the normalized semantic AST is frozen BEFORE surface syntax, and the formatter + migration tool ship BEFORE syntax stability is declared — so early adopters' sources can always be mechanically carried across surface revisions. START_HERE PR 15b (a review-5 insert; this deliverable previously had no owning PR).\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Deterministic CML formatter defined over the normalized semantic AST.\n- Migration tool rewriting existing CML sources across surface-syntax revisions.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Formatting is idempotent and semantics-preserving — a formatted or migrated source elaborates to the same semantic model identity — across the replicated-register model and the Wave 0 CML ports.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nSyntax churn without a migration tool would strand the Phase C/D corpus ports; identity-preservation makes churn free.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-c","cml"],"parent":"bn-1fd","size":"m","title":"PR 15b — CML formatter and migration tool [G5]"}	blake3:1iPnqTLs71SisPy6rMfhYnJRkILRKS0CdmX_nABO7jY
1785275510723996	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwLQ		item.create	bn-z2l	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase C's opening PR (plan §21.1): the §9 incremental semantic database, built or adopted per the PR 22a ADR (hard dep — PR 23 may not merge before PR 22a). START_HERE PR 23.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nContent-addressed queries for parsing, model construction, property automata, exploration, context, and diff. Classify reuse edges as Exact/Validated/Conservative/Experimental.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Property-only edit does not rebuild unrelated extraction.\n- [ ] Model action edit invalidates the reachable graph and dependent context (invalidation cone matches declared dependency classes).\n- [ ] Phase C owner/team row filled (merge requirement, §21.1).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nInteractive latency (G5, docs/34 table) is impossible without trustworthy reuse; the four edge classes are how reuse stays honest (mismatches quarantine the CLASS, not the world — plan §9.5).\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-c","incremental"],"parent":"bn-1fd","size":"l","title":"PR 23 — Incremental query database v0 [G5]"}	blake3:qfPH_3uatlFpLYMCfti7X6l79XpIlOpV1wiJmDdu4IE
1785275510845761	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwLg		item.create	bn-1ay	{"description":"# Context & Background\nRenamed from \"clean-build Tribunal\" — Tribunal now refers exclusively to the TLA+ corpus oracle harness (plan §9.5). The audit continuously compares incremental against clean-build results at a sampled rate derived from §8.6's confidence target. START_HERE PR 24. Register row: sub-file incremental trust (research/27; Phase C ratification deadline).\n\n# Implementation Approach\nRandomly and deterministically compare incremental and clean results; minimize invalidation mismatches; class-scoped quarantine on mismatch (RFC 0030's auditability classes, absorbed from §9.5).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] An intentionally broken dependency edge is caught and quarantined.\n- [ ] G5 bullet holds: incremental results continuously match clean builds under the audit.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe audit is the empirical backstop for every reuse claim; kill criterion (research/27): if dependency capture can't be trusted below file/module granularity, fall back to module-granularity invalidation.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-c","incremental"],"parent":"bn-1fd","size":"m","title":"PR 24 — Incremental Parity Audit [G5]"}	blake3:g2p5oIZJ5OO3uGvBofDs2ZPvMMtgADkGv3iWtVnqI6Y
1785275510961980	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwLw		item.create	bn-2yb	{"description":"# Context & Background\nEditor integration over snapshots — never over mutable daemon state. START_HERE PR 25.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nCML/source diagnostics, semantic hover, go-to correspondence, code lenses, intent-change preview over snapshots.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Unsaved buffer overlays create explicit snapshots and never mutate daemon state implicitly.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe edit/check/explain loop (G5 latency table) is lived in the editor; intent-change preview surfaces G3 semantics at authoring time instead of at promotion time.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-c","ux"],"parent":"bn-1fd","size":"m","title":"PR 25 — LSP authoring adapter [G5]"}	blake3:eqCoOlOu8Z7X_A-ZAR3qIyaKar_3qI6Q6w24OXD0DaU
1785275511072793	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwMA		item.create	bn-3w1	{"description":"# Context & Background\ncontinuum-sarif per plan §17.3 — CI/code-review ecosystem integration. START_HERE PR 25a. SARIF is a REPORT PROJECTION, not a proof format (evidence handles link back to the real artifacts).\n\n# Implementation Approach\nSource-located, deduplicated diagnostics with artifact URIs; stable rule IDs and severity; code flows; evidence handles.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The replicated-register failure exports schema-valid SARIF with stable rule IDs across reruns; every result carries an evidence handle.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nLets Continuum verdicts land in existing review tooling without pretending the SARIF file itself is evidence.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-c","ux"],"parent":"bn-1fd","size":"s","title":"PR 25a — SARIF exporter [G5]"}	blake3:BANt-yi5S0KJC5Lq_RJPnHhEV1_pmxWRNdEC8EuRZMI
1785275511186955	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwMQ		item.create	bn-21g	{"description":"# Context & Background\nDebug Adapter Protocol projection of the PR 19 causal debugger. START_HERE PR 26.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nMap Continuum debugger state to DAP threads, frames, scopes, variables, breakpoints, stepping; expose custom causal operations (branch, why-enabled/blocked) as DAP custom requests.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A VS Code-compatible client can inspect and branch the replicated-register failure.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nCausal debugging in a familiar surface is the adoption wedge for working engineers who will never learn a bespoke debugger UI.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-c","debugger","ux"],"parent":"bn-1fd","size":"m","title":"PR 26 — DAP adapter [G5]"}	blake3:kGp629E6EbuuTQmNEhFAdpAWMja75CXTZd_RZ8Lzg-w
1785275511301845	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwMg		item.create	bn-3b0	{"description":"# Context & Background\nAdoption top-of-funnel (plan §21 Phase C): observe an UNMODIFIED tokio program with scrupulously honest non-claims. Upgraded to the full instrumentation lane in Phase F. START_HERE PR 26a (review-5 insert).\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Journal observable lifecycle, channel, and time events from an unmodified tokio program.\n- Opaque-effect inventory for everything not observable.\n- Assurance envelopes capped at `Observed` status with every claim dimension `Unsupported` — no controlled-semantics claim.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A tokio example produces a semantic journal and an envelope capped at Observed.\n- [ ] No claim dimension can exceed Unsupported through this lane (fail-closed cap, tested).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nLets real tokio users see their systems in Continuum's terms on day one, without the plan ever lying about what that observation proves — the bridge Phase F's migrations walk across.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-c","adapter"],"parent":"bn-1fd","size":"m","title":"PR 26a — Read-only tokio observation lane [G5]"}	blake3:xOVWlbdY5SPd0ct5fiLx30VlC7nEEaY9y6p8YZigUVA
1785275511411682	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwMw		item.create	bn-16v	{"description":"# Context & Background\nModel Context Protocol surface for third-party agents — curated, capability-checked projection of the native protocol (plan §17). START_HERE PR 27.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nExpose curated native operations with explicit handles, deterministic schemas/order, result bounds, and capability checks.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Two subagents share one workspace/intent but use isolated debugger/proof handles without session coupling.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMulti-agent workflows (Phase F workbench) and ecosystem agents get the same fail-closed surface as the first-party client — no privileged side door.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-c","aci","protocol"],"parent":"bn-1fd","size":"m","title":"PR 27 — MCP adapter [G2]"}	blake3:6rgL5GLKU0v0OnC8oztIYDEeTsd8Ua9wYEj-r07nJWw
1785275511519414	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwNA		item.create	bn-2y2	{"description":"# Context & Background\nFirst corpus tranche: the 29 Wave 0/1 TLA+ families, pinned to tlaplus/Examples@91c22ea… (plan §21 Phase C). START_HERE PR 27a (review-5 insert). Parity levels per corpus/tla-examples/PARITY_LEVELS.md; Phase C caps at P2 (bounded semantic parity) — 14 families required at P4 and 3 at P3 need Phase D proof/liveness machinery, so their raises are deferred (Phase D bone).\n\n# Implementation Approach\nPort each family at min(required, P2): native model (CML where the Finite fragment suffices, programmatic otherwise), expected verdict and state facts. Corpus release checkpoint 0.1 closes at Phase C entry.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All 29 families check deterministically in CI at min(required, P2).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe corpus is the external ground truth (Tribunal); G9's interaction parity across 80 families in Phase F builds directly on these ports.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-c","corpus"],"parent":"bn-1fd","size":"l","title":"PR 27a — Wave 0/1 corpus at min(required, P2) [G9]"}	blake3:yoj-NsoEplTu7sBaIJRGyjwdVLXQ-Ia4X7KM082VBzw
1785275511634743	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwNQ		item.create	bn-10y	{"description":"# Context & Background\nTwo-track usability discipline (plan §21.1): FORMATIVE think-aloud sessions (~5 participants per §13.1 persona) run each phase from Phase C onward and feed docs/34 — explicitly non-binding on the SUMMATIVE G8 study, which is preregistered (expanded docs/48, authored as a Phase E deliverable) and executed in Phase F. START_HERE PR 27b (review-5 insert).\n\n# Implementation Approach\n- Formative instruments for the edit/check/explain loop, usable from Phase C on.\n- Begin authoring the expanded docs/48 preregistration: three human-executed docs/34 acceptance workflows (new model; existing Rust system; review), two cohorts (Rust newcomers on the deterministic/causal workflow; distributed-systems experts on real failures), raw-trace baseline comparator, cohort sizes, instruments, pass thresholds. (Publication is the Phase E bone; the summative study is the Phase F bone.)\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Formative instruments in use from Phase C, results feeding docs/34.\n- [ ] Preregistration draft exists and is on track for Phase E publication.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nPreregistration is how G8 avoids the trap of grading a usability study against thresholds invented after seeing the data; the formative/summative split lets design iterate without contaminating the graded study.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-c","ux","research"],"parent":"bn-1fd","size":"s","title":"PR 27b — G8 formative-session instruments and preregistration authoring [G8]"}	blake3:DqzQ_NoAixQTpbSi-jmG569GT-IAuxfCaEze4fW_a4M
1785275511751655	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwNg		item.create	bn-3hd	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase C deliverable (plan §21): the proof Context Pack FORMAT and compiler plumbing land in Phase C, but the proof-worker efficacy criterion (\"proof Context Packs improve proof-worker success/cost\") is G6's and is evaluated in Phase D against the PR 28 service. This split was made explicit in review 5 to stop Phase C from implicitly owning a criterion it cannot evaluate.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nExtend the PR 11 Context Pack compiler with proof-target packs: goal state, relevant hypotheses/lemmas, failed-tactic context, theorem/axiom manifest references. Format aligned with the context-pack schema (nine-dimension envelope mirror).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Proof packs compile deterministically for the PR 4a seed-theorem failures.\n- [ ] Format consumed by PR 28's relevant-context extraction without revision (the Phase D efficacy evaluation needs no format churn).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nKeeps the G6 efficacy evaluation honest: format risk retired in C, effectiveness measured in D against a real service.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-c","proof","aci"],"parent":"bn-1fd","size":"m","title":"Proof Context Pack format and compiler plumbing [G6 prep]"}	blake3:1E6O6wA5897hJg1NKG6V_ZdzsA_rtTU7as1x9Cd1BMc
1785275511854075	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwNw		item.create	bn-qgk	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPlan §21 (Phase D bullet) maps corpus release checkpoints to phases: 0.1 closes at PHASE C ENTRY; 0.2 in Phase D; 0.5 in Phase E; 1.0 (all 80) in Phase F with G9; 1.x tracks post-G9 upstream additions as maintenance outside the phase ladder.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Checkpoint 0.1's declared family set (per corpus/tla-examples inventory and PARITY_LEVELS.md) checks green in CI at Phase C entry, recorded against the pinned tlaplus/Examples commit.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nCheckpoints make corpus progress a dated, auditable ladder instead of a Phase F cliff.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-c","corpus"],"parent":"bn-1fd","size":"xs","title":"Corpus release checkpoint 0.1 (closes at Phase C entry)"}	blake3:jovI3cI-W6zyzZ76MusVq8Y3vQg8CT5P-QFcPYEPg6c
1785275511972954	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwOA		item.create	bn-lb8	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase D's opening PR (plan §21.1): the proof SERVICE over the environment pinned in PR 4a. Closes re-homed G0-DX-11 (proof-service isolation: concurrent Lean epochs, malicious inputs, cancellation → deterministic isolated results and resource bounds). START_HERE PR 28.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nPer-request isolation and cancellation; goals; diagnostics; receipts with theorem/axiom manifests; relevant-context extraction consuming the Phase C proof-pack format. (Lean pinning and T0/T1 are PR 4a, NOT here.)\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Concurrent requests across Lean epochs are isolated and cancellable with deterministic results.\n- [ ] Every proof receipt carries theorem and axiom manifests with zero unapproved axioms.\n- [ ] Phase D owner/team row filled (merge requirement, §21.1).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nG6's doctrine — agent proof repair accepted ONLY by kernel — needs an isolated, cancellable service boundary; failure consequence per the matrix was \"no remote proof lane\".\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-d","proof"],"parent":"bn-1db","size":"l","title":"PR 28 — Lean proof service and proof Context Pack [G6; closes re-homed G0-DX-11]"}	blake3:y9PrrSYVvkL5KDGlmdUygG7v41sM7W6hGE_8zGZoAEc
1785275512087697	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwOQ		item.create	bn-c4q	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase D deliverable (plan §21): extend the PR 19 causal debugger from safety counterexamples to fairness/liveness — lasso/cycle evidence, fairness-constraint views, why-not-eventually derivations. Register row: liveness-preserving reduction (research/04, research/13 — lane to be opened; soundness gate: property-directed reduction proven preserving for the full property class it is applied to, FAIR CYCLES INCLUDED, or disabled; Phase D ratification deadline). Fallback: unreduced liveness with explicit cost banner; batch expectations stated in the Phase D exit.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A liveness counterexample (fair cycle) on a corpus protocol is inspectable and branchable like a safety trace.\n- [ ] Reduction is either proven preserving for the applied property class or disabled (fail-closed, docs/08 R04 pattern).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe Phase D exit demands liveness EVIDENCE on a nontrivial corpus protocol — a checker verdict alone doesn't close G6's explanation obligations.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-d","debugger"],"parent":"bn-1db","size":"l","title":"Fairness/liveness debugger [G6]"}	blake3:L7Vea7laEYAjpkP6-Gs_yew4ezQ7x2oiZHO0tn2NIg4
1785275512204148	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwOg		item.create	bn-1fl	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase D deliverable (plan §21): synthesize candidate invariants and ranking functions to support proofs (safety induction, liveness termination arguments). Candidates are proposals — only kernel/Lean acceptance promotes them (the §11 authority rules apply to synthesized artifacts exactly as to agent patches).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Synthesized invariant closes a safety proof obligation on at least one corpus protocol.\n- [ ] Synthesized ranking function closes a liveness obligation on at least one protocol.\n- [ ] Rejected candidates leave typed evidence, not silence.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nFeeds the Phase D exit and later co-synthesis in Forge (Phase E co-synthesis of algorithm/invariant/ranking/abstraction builds on this machinery).\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-d","proof","engine"],"parent":"bn-1db","size":"m","title":"Invariant/ranking synthesis [G6]"}	blake3:BKdLni9AGk1kwLxD1JgQ1tkPM-49-AUBqx2zLbYlpWs
1785275512311457	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwOw		item.create	bn-20y	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase D deliverable (plan §21): agent-driven repair of broken proofs (after semantic changes) with kernel-only acceptance. Register row: proof repair (research/28; vs source/LSP-loop baseline, margin fixed at ratification; KILL if repair proposes WEAKENING — the reward-hacking failure mode in proof space; Phase D ratification deadline). Fallback: context packs + human proof work.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A theorem broken by a benign semantic change is repaired agent-side and re-accepted by the kernel with manifests intact.\n- [ ] A repair that weakens the theorem statement is classified as an intent-level change and blocked (never silently accepted).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nProof maintenance cost is the classic reason formal methods rot in real repos; bounded agent repair under kernel acceptance is the plan's answer.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-d","proof"],"parent":"bn-1db","size":"m","title":"Proof repair service [G6]"}	blake3:NFCVgl04HbKmpVuTWkygjVr6rukMYklLNNj5nWsJTwc
1785275512413249	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwPA		item.create	bn-3p2	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase D deliverable (plan §21): receipts binding abstract-model evidence to concrete code via the §16/PR 17 correspondence — the artifact that says \"this proof is ABOUT this code\", with epochs and manifests, verifiable independently like promotion receipts.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The replicated register (or a corpus protocol) carries a refinement receipt linking model-level safety/liveness evidence to the concrete implementation.\n- [ ] Receipt survives independent re-verification; stale correspondence (either side drifts) invalidates it fail-closed.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"Real-code refinement\" is the load-bearing phrase of the Phase D exit — without it, proofs are about models nobody runs.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-d","proof","evidence"],"parent":"bn-1db","size":"m","title":"Refinement receipts [G6]"}	blake3:cd1ZTOWNKrNQ9nJH325LNWsS8trQo5mLSqvQG8BY5ns
1785275512550300	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwPQ		item.create	bn-1rb	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase D deliverable (plan §21): a CML model of continuumd's OWN task/continuation lifecycle and INV-017 publication protocol, bound to the daemon implementation via §16 correspondence machinery and checked in CI from Phase D onward. A daemon concurrency defect found this way is recorded as G4-class evidence of product value.\n\n# Reasoning\nDogfooding at the strongest level: the verifier verifies itself where it is most concurrent and most trusted (task lifecycle, atomic publication). Also a standing regression net for PRs 2/6's G0 evidence.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] CML model of task/continuation lifecycle + INV-017 exists in the Finite fragment.\n- [ ] Bound to the daemon via correspondence; CI-checked from Phase D onward.\n- [ ] Any defect found is written up as G4-class product-value evidence.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\n\"We use it on ourselves and it found real bugs\" is the most credible adoption argument Phase F can carry into migration conversations.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-d","proof","kernel"],"parent":"bn-1db","size":"l","title":"Self-application: CML model of continuumd bound in CI [G6/G4-class evidence]"}	blake3:xzchKQK3qIVphM2e-J6tVheqnbBjPKE7rS5NtsZrnEo
1785275512672054	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwPg		item.create	bn-2dv	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase C delivered the 29 Wave 0/1 families capped at P2; the 14 families required at P4 and 3 at P3 needed Phase D's proof and liveness machinery (plan §21). This raises them to full required parity per corpus/tla-examples/PARITY_LEVELS.md.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All 17 deferred families reach their required parity (P3/P4) deterministically in CI.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDirect G9 progress and the first at-scale exercise of the proof/liveness machinery beyond hand-picked protocols.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-d","corpus"],"parent":"bn-1db","size":"m","title":"Wave 0/1 corpus raises to full required parity (P3/P4) [G9]"}	blake3:xLuCY7Wmuvb-YxAcW-Uq9HM-AjU2v_nPpNfi6gUEWQw
1785275512790134	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwPw		item.create	bn-93e	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase D deliverable (plan §21): corpus Waves 2–3 ported at required parity; corpus release checkpoint 0.2 closes in Phase D. (Checkpoint 0.5 — Waves 0–2 at required parity plus ≥5 named P5 exemplars — closes in Phase E; 1.0 with all 80 closes in Phase F with G9.)\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Waves 2–3 families check deterministically in CI at required parity.\n- [ ] Checkpoint 0.2 recorded against the pinned corpus commit.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nKeeps corpus growth on the phase ladder so G9 in Phase F is a closing step, not a mountain.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-d","corpus"],"parent":"bn-1db","size":"l","title":"Corpus Waves 2–3 at required parity; checkpoint 0.2 [G9]"}	blake3:yAEHxt6_BHVHMcKRte_3NjHhflwp4WNj6WUBon7JBWc
1785275636578149	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwQA		item.create	bn-3ix	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase E's opening PR (plan §21.1): Continuum Forge v0 — synthesis under verification, finite and honest. Closes re-homed G0-DX-07 (Forge non-vacuity; artifact shape from the Phase A spike, engine evidence here per plan §22). START_HERE PR 29. Forge may not be imported by the verifier (dependency-island rule).\n\n# Implementation Approach\nTyped holes; finite grammar enumeration; exact counterexample feedback; safety plus progress/non-vacuity objectives; candidate archive; independent candidate verification (candidates are proposals — the ordinary evidence path grades them, Forge grades nothing itself).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The acknowledgement-guard task synthesizes `synced`, rejects never-ack/`false` (vacuous) candidates, and independently verifies the result (plan §25's from-scratch demonstration: ack_guard = synced).\n- [ ] Phase E owner/team row filled (merge requirement, §21.1).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nNon-vacuity is Forge's soul: without a progress objective, synthesis returns the guard that never acknowledges. The spike proved the objective shape; this makes it an engine.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-e","forge"],"parent":"bn-4rm","size":"l","title":"PR 29 — Forge finite CEGIS v0 [G7]"}	blake3:xjGK1dEyETeNcvObEGXmTGJ-mV_AcIVZw5rOAcOBIHM
1785275636694633	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwQQ		item.create	bn-13k	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase E deliverable (plan §21): scale CEGIS beyond naive enumeration — counterexample generalization and interpretation-reduction so candidate spaces prune semantically rather than syntactically.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Measurably fewer verifier calls than naive enumeration on the rediscovery suite, with identical winners.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe G7 register row's kill criteria (research/29) include \"joint space overwhelms coupled-feedback gains\" — this is the mitigation lane.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-e","forge"],"parent":"bn-4rm","size":"m","title":"CEGIS/interpretation reduction [G7]"}	blake3:-i2eiOAeWQFuV1wTDwWp4AkDUeqtmjhu9Gs5cQLLV2k
1785275636808171	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwQg		item.create	bn-fq8	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase E deliverable (plan §21): a quality-diversity candidate archive with SEMANTIC, not merely syntactic, spread (G7 bullet). Register kill (research/30): QD discovers nothing beyond multi-objective Pareto search on known benchmarks → fall back to a lightweight semantic-diversity archive.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Archive diversity measured semantically (behavioral distance), not by AST shape.\n- [ ] Hidden-variant generalization: archived candidates generalize to withheld variants (G7 bullet).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nDiversity is what makes Forge an explorer instead of a memorizer; the fallback keeps the feature honest if QD under-delivers.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-e","forge"],"parent":"bn-4rm","size":"m","title":"Quality-diversity archive [G7]"}	blake3:npkenppDF1LTpcgTNhYPo543CgJk6aaHcxFflJ875PQ
1785275636918367	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwQw		item.create	bn-1x7	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase E deliverable (plan §21): joint synthesis of the algorithm together with its proof artifacts (invariant, ranking function, abstraction), building on the Phase D invariant/ranking machinery. Register kills (research/29): proof-complexity objective biases toward trivial designs; abstractions overfit finite bounds; agent proposals dominate and cannot be reproduced by structured search.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] At least one behaviorally novel candidate, independently verified within the declared envelope (Phase E exit clause).\n- [ ] Unrealizability reported as DISTINCT from unknown, producing reusable evidence where supported (G7 bullets).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nCo-synthesis is the \"invention\" half of the product theorem — intent through invention AND proof.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-e","forge"],"parent":"bn-4rm","size":"l","title":"Co-synthesis of algorithm/invariant/ranking/abstraction [G7]"}	blake3:wrhGZGow0DjsOWT--Ii4mRUQSezA_HF1pWtS-nXHzCE
1785275637044928	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwRA		item.create	bn-2nr	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase E deliverable (plan §21): a promoted Forge candidate materializes into a model, a Rust/asupersync skeleton, and its proof obligations — synthesis output lands as reviewable code plus obligations, never as a black-box blob.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A promoted candidate materializes model + Rust skeleton + proof obligations (G7 bullet), all content-addressed and linked in the evidence graph.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMaterialized obligations are what keep synthesized code inside the same trust loop as human code.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-e","forge"],"parent":"bn-4rm","size":"m","title":"Materialization to asupersync skeleton [G7]"}	blake3:VL-EdAM88Q7qzHPY5wHpbMqYP6Ijek8vgTVc5-6rwMg
1785275637166488	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwRQ		item.create	bn-1uq	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase E deliverable (plan §21.1): the expanded docs/48 preregistration — three docs/34 human workflows (new model; existing Rust system; review), two cohorts (Rust newcomers, deterministic/causal workflow; distributed-systems experts, real failures), raw-trace baseline comparator, cohort sizes, instruments, and pass thresholds — is PUBLISHED before the summative study runs (Phase F). The fourth docs/34 workflow (agent repair) is covered by the G2 ACI ablation and ContinuumBench, not the human study. Also the ratification vehicle for the explanation-science register row (research/34 thresholds are fixed in this document).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Expanded docs/48 published with fixed workflows, cohorts, sizes, instruments, thresholds, and baseline.\n- [ ] G8 bullet satisfied: results will be graded ONLY against these fixed thresholds.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nPreregistration converts the usability claim from marketing into science; it is also the explanation-science lane's ratification.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-e","ux","research"],"parent":"bn-4rm","size":"s","title":"Publish expanded docs/48 G8 preregistration"}	blake3:QyEqkci8-KA4fwUab3cLw4uJNf2oKw19eV_AQ1usk6w
1785275637281402	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwRg		item.create	bn-1qy	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPlan §21: checkpoint 0.5 — Waves 0–2 at required parity plus ≥5 P5 exemplar families, now designated BY NAME in validated-examples.csv — closes in Phase E.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Waves 0–2 at required parity in CI; the five-plus named P5 exemplars reach P5.\n- [ ] Checkpoint recorded against the pinned corpus commit.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nP5 exemplars (full interaction parity) are the templates the remaining 80-family push in Phase F copies.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-e","corpus"],"parent":"bn-4rm","size":"s","title":"Corpus release checkpoint 0.5 (Waves 0–2 + ≥5 named P5 exemplars)"}	blake3:P89UAnu66oNL5G_buQ_GIgXLjIPv5LHwosbtbAyB32c
1785275637400698	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwRw		item.create	bn-m21	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe capstone of the trustworthy loop and the gatekeeper for Phase F (whose opening PR is the first PR sequenced after PR 30 closes). START_HERE PR 30. Gates G2/G4 belong to Phases A/B — closed gates; this PR attaches hardening regression evidence per §21.1's hardening rule, at full-loop scale.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nGive an advanced coding agent ONLY: repository snapshot; protected intent; failing Context Pack; native operations; bounded budget. Require it to diagnose, patch, evaluate, and request promotion.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The complete receipt is produced without terminal parsing or human repair guidance.\n- [ ] All operations and evidence are replayable.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThis is plan §25's \"brutally concrete\" demonstration run for real — the moment the product theorem stops being a diagram.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-e","aci"],"parent":"bn-4rm","size":"m","title":"PR 30 — End-to-end agent benchmark [G2, G4]"}	blake3:Ctm7Hd_tB6B7iRZMMh_6eALbtK3yw3tg_DaF9DqWcH0
1785275637538033	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwSA		item.create	bn-3ij	{"description":"# Context & Background\nEvery Phase F deliverable sits in START_HERE's deliberately deferred set, so Phase F's opening PR is deliberately unsequenced: \"the first PR sequenced after PR 30 closes; it must be designated here before Phase F work begins\" (START_HERE resourcing section). Filling the owner/team row is that PR's merge requirement.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Opening PR designated in START_HERE's resourcing table.\n- [ ] Phase F owner/team row filled; validator-derived program status for F no longer BLOCKED.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nKeeps the phase-governance invariant (no ownerless phase claims progress) intact through the least-sequenced phase.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-f","governance"],"parent":"bn-22h","size":"xs","title":"Designate Phase F opening PR and fill owner/team row (§21.1)"}	blake3:t3DRH9mpAlZewxst9EDseL64d7gIl24KaXeboteoJKM
1785275637669477	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwSQ		item.create	bn-3fm	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase F deliverable (plan §21): evidence from PRODUCTION executions — partial-order observations classified against intent, returning valid pass/fail/inconclusive (INV-008, a G10 bullet). Register row: production conformance (research/05, research/36; draft thresholds: faithful Lab reproduction of ≥70% of curated known incidents under injected telemetry loss and clock uncertainty; always-on overhead ≤1%; ≥2× reduction in surviving symmetry orbits per synthesized probe set; Phase F ratification deadline). Kill: most target properties monitorable only as Inconclusive on two real systems, or overhead budgets unmeetable. Fallback: Lab-replay evidence only.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Production evidence returns valid classifications on a real system; Inconclusive is honest, not padding.\n- [ ] Ratified thresholds met or fallback adopted with the register updated.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nProduction evidence is what lets migrated projects retire bespoke DST (G10's actual bar) rather than run both forever.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-f","adapter","engine"],"parent":"bn-22h","size":"l","title":"Production partial-order evidence [G10]"}	blake3:nmH1X26Ty-dmVrAfLU2a-X2FP_FO9sX6L2L3ZquK9Ps
1785275637795208	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwSg		item.create	bn-3my	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase F deliverable (plan §21): upgrade the Phase C read-only tokio lane (PR 26a, capped at Observed/Unsupported) to a real instrumentation lane: instrumentation SYNTHESIS, fault-window replay where monitorable, and correspondingly bounded assurance envelopes. An adoption bridge — explicitly NOT a claim of controlled semantics.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Instrumentation synthesis produces working probes for a real tokio system.\n- [ ] Fault-window replay works where monitorable; envelopes upgrade exactly as far as the instrumentation justifies, no further.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMost candidate migrations run tokio, not asupersync; this lane is how they onboard without a rewrite, with envelopes that never overstate what observation proves.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-f","adapter"],"parent":"bn-22h","size":"l","title":"Foreign-runtime instrumentation lane: tokio upgrade [G10]"}	blake3:QG6JGHsIpa9DtCB-UmafH4bm7Ht4ZxnIHct5N9hSJCA
1785275637929040	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwSw		item.create	bn-1wf	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase F deliverable (plan §21): remote execution for proof and verification workloads — the scale-out lane deferred until the loop was trustworthy (START_HERE deferred set: distributed model checking, cloud control plane). Isolation/cancellation semantics inherit from PR 28 (G6) and the task service (PR 6); identity and authority cross the wire unchanged.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Remote workers produce identical artifacts to local execution (determinism across the boundary).\n- [ ] Authority/capability checks hold remotely; no remote path can promote evidence an unprivileged local client couldn't.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nOperating cost acceptability is a G10 bullet; remote workers are the cost/scale lever.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-f","protocol","proof"],"parent":"bn-22h","size":"l","title":"Remote proof/verification workers [G10]"}	blake3:98JeDADSaPXu9JugyD3e39o_ksgcdj5hf_-Huvg0_YE
1785275638046835	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwTA		item.create	bn-1z1	{"description":"# Context & Background\nG9: 80 validated TLA+ families at their declared parity level (corpus/tla-examples/PARITY_LEVELS.md), measured per plan §19.4's HELD-OUT discipline — this closes re-homed G0-DX-15 (benchmark leakage: hidden mutation splits, source-hash isolation, deterministic grading). Corpus release checkpoint 1.0. Every family carries its interaction artifacts: native model, expected verdict and state facts, meaningful explanation, failure/mutation task, proof/refinement support where applicable, and an agent benchmark artifact — INTERACTION parity per B23, not a family count.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All 80 families at declared parity, deterministic in CI.\n- [ ] Interaction artifacts present per family; held-out gaming suite evaluated ONCE, here (G3's development/held-out split).\n- [ ] DX-15 leakage checks pass (no answer leakage; deterministic grading; held-out behavior variants).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThe corpus is the external referee: 80 independently-authored protocols at interaction parity is the anti-cherry-picking proof.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-f","corpus"],"parent":"bn-22h","size":"l","title":"All 80 corpus families at declared parity — corpus 1.0 [G9]"}	blake3:Ti9pd006I3l_MFSa3iEP_zcWLbuBHs6QmqLYchlnkNA
1785275638176637	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwTQ		item.create	bn-1vo	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase F deliverable (plan §21, §19): the public agent benchmark. HARD PRECONDITION: the corpus per-family redistribution audit (PR 0 program decisions) must exist before any public benchmark release (§21.1) — wired as a dep in this graph. Development/held-out separation per §19.4 with source-hash isolation.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Public release with documented task format (continuumbench-task schema), grading, and leakage discipline.\n- [ ] Redistribution audit satisfied for every released family.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nA public benchmark is a G10 bullet and the ecosystem's invitation to beat us honestly — the same anti-gaming machinery graded our own agents first.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-f","corpus","aci"],"parent":"bn-22h","size":"m","title":"ContinuumBench public release [G10]"}	blake3:XtZrKCE8W9VYcCmefehUvoR0kV24AwtWVlrq_H_xu8Q
1785275638305720	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwTg		item.create	bn-236	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe summative G8 study (plan §21.1), run in Phase F against the docs/48 preregistration published in Phase E; also closes re-homed G0-DX-09. Covers both cohorts: Rust newcomers completing the deterministic/causal workflow, and distributed-systems experts correctly diagnosing REAL failures, against a raw-trace baseline.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria (G8 bullets, graded only against preregistered thresholds)\n- [ ] Explanation beats raw-trace baseline on diagnosis accuracy and time.\n- [ ] Assurance confidence is CALIBRATED, not merely no worse than baseline (miscalibration is the DX-09 failure signal).\n- [ ] Progressive disclosure reaches exact artifacts; no critical workflow requires color, a rendered graph, or formal-methods folklore.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nG8 is where \"humans understand explanations better than raw traces\" stops being a hypothesis; kill consequence is redesign explanation UX (research/34 fallback: §12.1 levels 1–2 only, no adequacy claim).\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-f","ux","research"],"parent":"bn-22h","size":"l","title":"Preregistered G8 usability study — execute and analyze [G8]"}	blake3:pwWBPQnyKz1XfUPUZkEAiarJSEi35QY3MG94QjMdD2M
1785275638432295	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwTw		item.create	bn-iyk	{"description":"# Context & Background\nPhase F deliverable (plan §21): concurrent agent swarms (planner/repairer/prover/reviewer) working one workspace under enforced authority — the full realization of what G0-DX-12 spiked (authority TABLE) and the §11.7/RFC 0038 register row demands beyond the spike: linearized promotion, CAS status transitions, conflict materialization under concurrent writers (docs/53 boundary: \"authority table only, not enforcement\"). Fallback if enforcement can't ratify: single-writer evidence graph; agents coordinate through one integrator role.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Concurrent adversarial swarm cannot create an unauthorized intent edge (DX-12 pass condition, now under real concurrency).\n- [ ] G5's saturating-background-swarm latency measurement uses this machinery.\n- [ ] Zero known paths for an unprivileged agent to promote false evidence (G10 bullet, red-team corpus per research/35).\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nMulti-agent throughput is the product's scale story; authority enforcement is what keeps swarms from being an attack surface.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-f","aci","security"],"parent":"bn-22h","size":"l","title":"Multi-agent workbench [G10]"}	blake3:CHShsC2kirSKwgxmqP98f9MZqkd7InU8BJnkSE57Nko
1785275638548437	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwUA		item.create	bn-36z	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe Phase F exit and G10's core criterion, stated identically in plan §21, §22 G10, and docs/52 G10 (reconciled in one commit per §22's rule): Continuum replaces bespoke DST plus separate TLA+ workflow in at least TWO materially different real systems; at least two migrated projects stop requiring a separate TLA+ workflow for normal development; agent-driven repair is used on real changes under review. Kill criterion (plan §24, R01 adoption checkpoint) watches this lane. Scope guard: migrations recruited within the one-workspace-snapshot scope — cross-repo federation is a 1.0 non-goal.\n\n# Implementation Approach\nRecruit candidates early (the PR 26a observed lane is the funnel); migrate via the Phase F instrumentation lane or native asupersync adoption; document assurance envelopes per system (G10 bullet); track operating cost (G10 bullet).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Two materially different real projects remove bespoke DST infrastructure.\n- [ ] Both stop requiring a separate TLA+ workflow for normal development.\n- [ ] Agent-driven repair used on real changes under review in these projects.\n\n# How This Serves Project Goals\nThis IS the definition of success (plan §23): everything else in the graph exists so this bone can close.\n","kind":"task","labels":["phase-f","governance"],"parent":"bn-22h","size":"l","title":"G10 adoption: two real-system migrations replace DST + TLA+ workflow"}	blake3:n8qL3a5cketisUH8R12bnn0z2HQS0ZZihY1RDQ5NPDo
1785275638693338	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwUQ		item.create	bn-hii	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§24.5 row: general context compilation (§6). Ablation design per research/25 (raw trace vs pack on the agent benchmark); the WIN MARGIN must be fixed numerically at ratification. Kill (research/32): compact packs repeatedly induce incorrect repairs despite preservation checks. Fallback: plain causal slice + expansion. Deadline: before Phase A opens (wired as dep on PR 0).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Numeric threshold fixed in the research note; register quotes it verbatim with matching quote-id markers in both files; validator check_register_rows passes quote identity.\n","kind":"task","labels":["research","phase-a"],"parent":"bn-j09","size":"s","title":"Ratify: general context compilation (research/25/32/33) — Phase A deadline"}	blake3:ejXZEW3-T1nnvDD-g7s-fhehGpgerJZTqDlNAU4xJBU
1785275638817367	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwUg		item.create	bn-2fj	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§24.5 row: ACI (B2, §10). Native ACI vs disciplined-shell baseline on the agent benchmark; success-rate and cost margins fixed at ratification. Kills (research/25): typed surface loses to disciplined shell; schema churn dominates agent cost; handles do not reduce invalid-action rate. Fallback: protocol redesign before freeze (G0-DX-10); MCP-only surface. Deadline: before Phase A opens. This row's margins are what PR 10's benchmark is graded against — ratify BEFORE the benchmark runs.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Margins fixed in research/25; verbatim quote + quote-id markers; validator passes.\n","kind":"task","labels":["research","phase-a","aci"],"parent":"bn-j09","size":"s","title":"Ratify: agent–computer interface (research/25) — Phase A deadline"}	blake3:qTKcx6BF-h6FrNkXRh_ndYF7j5GjUCBoBhBsAtV5FuM
1785275638949764	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwUw		item.create	bn-2bu	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§24.5 row: causal minimization (§6, §12). Draft threshold: replay-preserving core ≤10% of trace length on REAL (non-synthetic) failures. Kill: minimization cost dominates verification. Fallback: 1-minimal delta debugging only. Deadline: before Phase A opens. (The G0-DX-01 spike's 200→4 was synthetic — plan §25 explicitly bounds it; the ratified threshold governs real failures.)\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Threshold fixed in research/26; verbatim quote + quote-id markers; validator passes.\n","kind":"task","labels":["research","phase-a"],"parent":"bn-j09","size":"s","title":"Ratify: causal minimization (research/26) — Phase A deadline"}	blake3:d22x5Dux1MHjsU-pwE2DugdFplzkqteYYEw7oacSwwk
1785275639086756	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwVA		item.create	bn-2ba	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§24.5 row: neighborhood adequacy (§8.3). Lane to be OPENED (research/33 exists as a stub direction): hidden-variant catch rate of the §8.3 neighborhood on the docs/50-classified gaming corpus; ≥N mutations per attack class, N fixed at lane opening; target fixed at lane opening. Kill: hidden variants too easy to leak or too hard to grade independently. Fallback: fixed strategy-list neighborhood with per-receipt coverage disclosure and no adequacy claim. Deadline: before Phase B opens (dep on PR 14). Depends on the gaming corpus existing (Phase B bone).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Lane opened, N and catch-rate target fixed; verbatim quote + quote-id markers; validator passes.\n","kind":"task","labels":["research","phase-b"],"parent":"bn-j09","size":"s","title":"Ratify: neighborhood adequacy (research/33, lane opening) — Phase B deadline"}	blake3:8kBeuFvXbkJVa5diuvscxLypxJF9eeVXiKuXohNBuIs
1785275639217213	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwVQ		item.create	bn-2oy	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§24.5 row: workbench security (§18). Promotion gate per research/35: autonomous promotion remains DISABLED until no known unprivileged path can alter intent/evidence status or escape isolation; red-team corpus per research/35. Fallback: human-approved promotion only. Deadline: before Phase B opens (Phase B is where agent repair with promotion begins).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Gate condition and red-team corpus scope fixed in research/35; verbatim quote + quote-id markers; validator passes.\n","kind":"task","labels":["research","phase-b","security"],"parent":"bn-j09","size":"s","title":"Ratify: workbench security (research/35) — Phase B deadline"}	blake3:N5lQW6T-ybOwdWttxPmkYRi1ZqP4CaEYQIpMmKbHv9U
1785275639355391	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwVg		item.create	bn-1o2	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§24.5 row: multi-agent evidence graph enforcement (§11.7). Concurrent authority enforcement validated beyond the finite spike (docs/53: \"authority table only, not enforcement\"): linearized promotion, CAS status transitions, conflict materialization under concurrent writers. Fallback: single-writer evidence graph; agents coordinate through one integrator role. Deadline: before Phase B opens.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Enforcement validation criteria fixed (in RFC 0038's lane note); verbatim quote + quote-id markers; validator passes.\n","kind":"task","labels":["research","phase-b"],"parent":"bn-j09","size":"s","title":"Ratify: multi-agent evidence-graph enforcement (RFC 0038) — Phase B deadline"}	blake3:Ushf21BuwcVX0_s-PWPCXFmLoJ_j3010ASn8zvIPdco
1785275639487903	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwVw		item.create	bn-2tt	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§24.5 row: sub-file incremental trust (§9). Threshold: clean-mismatch rate at the §9.5 sampled audit rate (rate derives from §8.6's confidence target). Kill: dependency capture cannot be made trustworthy below file/module granularity. Fallback: module-granularity invalidation. Deadline: before Phase C opens (dep on PR 23).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Mismatch-rate threshold fixed in research/27; verbatim quote + quote-id markers; validator passes.\n","kind":"task","labels":["research","phase-c"],"parent":"bn-j09","size":"s","title":"Ratify: sub-file incremental trust (research/27) — Phase C deadline"}	blake3:vFBYNreOn-BeC2Rt0XcUQJ4zbbAZYy99lu4nDP8fbBw
1785275639592931	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwWA		item.create	bn-1cp	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§24.5 row: liveness-preserving reduction (§7.2). Lane to be opened. Soundness gate per research/04: property-directed reduction is PROVEN preserving (for the full property class it is applied to, fair cycles included) or disabled; speedup target and liveness corpus subset fixed at lane opening. Fallback: unreduced liveness with explicit cost banner; batch expectations stated in the Phase D exit. Deadline: before Phase D opens (dep on PR 28).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Lane opened with speedup target + corpus subset; verbatim quote + quote-id markers; validator passes.\n","kind":"task","labels":["research","phase-d"],"parent":"bn-j09","size":"s","title":"Ratify: liveness-preserving reduction (research/04+13, lane opening) — Phase D deadline"}	blake3:vHJ1KNO0UIFJcgRgX0k56ClwiAHaDWDnnppXzrDETx0
1785275639719170	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwWQ		item.create	bn-1y7	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§24.5 row: proof repair (§15.4). Vs source/LSP-loop baseline (qualitative; margin fixed at ratification). Kill: repair proposes weakening. Fallback: context packs + human proof work. Deadline: before Phase D opens.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Margin fixed in research/28; verbatim quote + quote-id markers; validator passes.\n","kind":"task","labels":["research","phase-d","proof"],"parent":"bn-j09","size":"s","title":"Ratify: proof repair (research/28) — Phase D deadline"}	blake3:PnvCeELlebantO9qOWbrIEEwVdoBOwgERwh43uiJvw4
1785275639866847	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwWg		item.create	bn-320	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§24.5 row: certificate overhead. Threshold (docs/31): checking ≤10% of search time for large finite proofs, or acceptable asynchronous CI latency. research/12 MUST gain a criteria section before this row leaves draft (review-5 added the draft section; ratification fixes it). Fallback per docs/31: the verified native checker remains the routine path; Lean validates the checker and sampled/full release artifacts. Deadline: before Phase D opens.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] research/12 criteria section ratified; verbatim quote + quote-id markers; validator passes.\n","kind":"task","labels":["research","phase-d","proof"],"parent":"bn-j09","size":"s","title":"Ratify: certificate overhead (research/12 criteria section) — Phase D deadline"}	blake3:a4P14FqdcjcxU9NeZdYYkrer_-Zpr3IEP1xsS-QM1rQ
1785275640008921	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwWw		item.create	bn-195	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§24.5 row: Forge co-synthesis + QD (§14). Rediscovery suite: named algorithms and count fixed at ratification. Kills (research/29): joint space overwhelms coupled-feedback gains; proof-complexity objective biases toward trivial designs; abstractions overfit finite bounds; agent proposals dominate and cannot be reproduced by structured search. Kill (research/30): QD discovers nothing beyond multi-objective Pareto search. Fallbacks: staged synthesis; lightweight semantic-diversity archive. Deadline: before Phase E opens (dep on PR 29).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Rediscovery suite named and counted in research/29/30; verbatim quote + quote-id markers; validator passes.\n","kind":"task","labels":["research","phase-e","forge"],"parent":"bn-j09","size":"s","title":"Ratify: Forge co-synthesis + QD (research/29/30) — Phase E deadline"}	blake3:R6Uc66F0xfP8vFKhNSVi-IWvzr-Ev4ubsPGhRl92PMw
1785275640144062	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwXA		item.create	bn-cs3	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§24.5 row: explanation science (§12). Diagnosis accuracy/time vs raw-trace baseline under the G8 instruments and Phase C formative sessions; thresholds fixed IN THE DOCS/48 PREREGISTRATION (the Phase E publication bone is the ratification vehicle). Kills (research/34): experts prefer raw output and newcomers gain no accuracy; assistance raises confidence faster than correctness. Fallback: §12.1 levels 1–2 only (outcome + causal core), no adequacy claim. Deadline: before Phase E opens.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Thresholds fixed in docs/48 + research/34; verbatim quote + quote-id markers; validator passes.\n","kind":"task","labels":["research","phase-e","ux"],"parent":"bn-j09","size":"s","title":"Ratify: explanation science (research/34, via docs/48) — Phase E deadline"}	blake3:YkejzDDBqHHsHfJ7K9RZdNmIn5MvPxjn-PVgQrGj3Lw
1785275640270562	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwXQ		item.create	bn-1pg	{"description":"# Context & Background\n§24.5 row: production conformance (Phase F). Draft thresholds: faithful Lab reproduction of ≥70% of curated known incidents under injected telemetry loss and clock uncertainty; always-on overhead ≤1%; ≥2× reduction in surviving symmetry orbits per synthesized probe set. Kill: most target properties monitorable only as Inconclusive on two real systems, or overhead budgets unmeetable. Fallback: Lab-replay evidence only. Deadline: before Phase F opens (dep on the Phase F opening-designation bone).\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Thresholds ratified in research/05/36; verbatim quote + quote-id markers; validator passes.\n","kind":"task","labels":["research","phase-f"],"parent":"bn-j09","size":"s","title":"Ratify: production conformance (research/05/36) — Phase F deadline"}	blake3:ZgHmTkvKFVdirFRoroObVRBfHZxzK5gbX7lvfVVbA60
1785275640399569	claude	itc:v3:AWL5JJk5zz5JJEAAAAAAQfqrXd76qgAAAAEwXg		item.create	bn-2z3	{"description":"# Context & Background\nThe §24.5 rows with no phase deadline ratify at their lane's opening, before any plan section consumes their output:\n- Exploration reduction (research/01; docs/31): order-of-magnitude reduction on a non-artificial subset without serious regression on dependent workloads — the \"explored classes\" DENOMINATOR is itself a proposed ratification to fix in research/01 first; observer-indexed variant (docs/31): median ≥5× on the observer-sensitive class, checker overhead <20%, zero mutation loss.\n- Bidirectional lenses (research/31): ambiguity rate on the drift corpus (the corpus is the lane deliverable); kill if most real mappings are too ambiguous or users mistake candidate synchronization for verified preservation (measured under G8 instruments). Fallback: get-only projection + drift detection.\n- Nominal/orbit-finite (research/14; docs/31) and sheaf composition (research/03; docs/31): docs/31's stated promote/kill pairs.\n- Also (per §24.5 closing paragraph): merge docs/31's remaining thresholds (cubical reduction ≥3× on ≥2 real protocol classes with a preservation theorem; semiring within 15% of specialized analyses with three production analyses sharing code; assumption synthesis on ≥5 liveness cases; abstraction discovery as a draft row pending quantitative ratification) into the register during the §25 pass so the program has exactly ONE lane authority; where docs/31 and a research note disagree, the note is corrected and cited.\nDefer rows (weak memory ADR-0032, timed/probabilistic ADR-0016, TLA+ importer) are exempt while their deferrals stand — no bones.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Each listed row either ratified at its lane opening (verbatim quote + quote-id markers) or its lane remains unopened with no plan section consuming its output.\n- [ ] docs/31 residual thresholds merged; register is the single lane authority.\n","kind":"task","labels":["research"],"parent":"bn-j09","size":"m","title":"Ratify at lane opening: exploration reduction, bidirectional lenses, nominal/orbit-finite, sheaf composition; merge docs/31 residual thresholds"}	blake3:zMk0AAhsSu6CgKb956QVqhFOv5HwqAdejptCOXwHzZk
